WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:06.560
OK.

00:00:06.560 --> 00:00:08.280
Let's get started.

00:00:08.280 --> 00:00:09.840
So welcome back, everyone.

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:13.320
So today we're going to
talk about parallelism.

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:15.320
And remember in
the last week, we

00:00:15.320 --> 00:00:20.200
introduced how to make a single
GPU go fast by writing kernels.

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:23.200
And we really looked
inside this GPU.

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:25.000
And this week,
we're going to talk

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:27.720
about how to leverage
multiple GPUs to make

00:00:27.720 --> 00:00:30.888
your code go even faster.

00:00:30.888 --> 00:00:32.680
So the picture you
should have in your head

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:34.400
is something like this.

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:37.760
So for the last week, we
focused on one of these boxes,

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:40.080
where you have your GPU.

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:44.100
Remember, you have your High
Bandwidth Memory, HBM, L2 cache,

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:51.680
L1 cache, registers, and a bunch
of streaming multiprocessors.

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:53.640
So now the picture
gets extended,

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:56.860
because instead of having
one GPU, you might have four,

00:00:56.860 --> 00:00:59.260
you might have 1,000 GPUs.

00:00:59.260 --> 00:01:01.120
And those are going
to be connected.

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:04.080
And I'll talk later
about how those GPUs are

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:06.048
going to get connected.

00:01:06.048 --> 00:01:07.840
And then you're going
to have to figure out

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:12.840
how to leverage all of this
compute to train models.

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:16.880
So in both cases, meaning
both in the single GPU case

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:20.280
and in the multi-GPU case,
as we'll talk about today,

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:23.260
the situation is kind of
similar if you zoom out,

00:01:23.260 --> 00:01:27.660
which is that the compute,
the arithmetic logic units,

00:01:27.660 --> 00:01:31.080
the tensor core, and so on,
is far away from your data.

00:01:31.080 --> 00:01:33.400
And far away for a single
GPU means all the way

00:01:33.400 --> 00:01:35.760
over here in HBM.

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:39.920
And now if you have multi GPUs,
the thing that you need here

00:01:39.920 --> 00:01:42.080
might be all the way
on a different GPU,

00:01:42.080 --> 00:01:45.200
and you're going to have to
shuffle that over somehow.

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:46.880
But the same
principles are going

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:49.520
to be the same,
because the game is

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:54.320
to orchestrate the computation
to try to avoid data transfer

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:55.280
bottlenecks.

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:57.880
It's very easy to
use a ton of GPUs,

00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:01.350
but it's hard to use
them effectively.

00:02:01.350 --> 00:02:04.090
So taking a little
bit of liberty,

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:06.710
we can think about the
generalized hierarchy

00:02:06.710 --> 00:02:13.750
where at the local
level next to the SMs

00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:18.190
a single node single GPU
where you have an L1 cache

00:02:18.190 --> 00:02:18.970
shared memory.

00:02:18.970 --> 00:02:20.470
This was the fastest.

00:02:20.470 --> 00:02:25.910
And then you had HBM, which
we lamented was so slow.

00:02:25.910 --> 00:02:30.590
But in this lecture, HBM is
going to be considered fast.

00:02:30.590 --> 00:02:33.870
Now we're going to think about
the single node multi-GPU

00:02:33.870 --> 00:02:36.390
setting where the GPUs
are going to be connected

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:38.950
via NVLink and NVSwitch.

00:02:38.950 --> 00:02:42.370
And then finally, the
multi-node and multi-GPU,

00:02:42.370 --> 00:02:44.230
where we have to
resort to InfiniBand

00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:49.370
and ethernet depending
on what network you have.

00:02:49.370 --> 00:02:54.790
So last week, we talked
about various tricks

00:02:54.790 --> 00:03:00.270
for in reducing memory
accesses, fusion and tiling.

00:03:00.270 --> 00:03:03.830
Read into shared memory,
do as much as you can,

00:03:03.830 --> 00:03:05.990
and then write it back out.

00:03:05.990 --> 00:03:07.590
And this week,
we're going to talk

00:03:07.590 --> 00:03:10.830
about how you can reduce
the amount of communication

00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:17.470
across GPUs by replicating
and sharding appropriately.

00:03:17.470 --> 00:03:21.230
So why do you do multi-GPUs?

00:03:21.230 --> 00:03:23.910
The obvious answer is,
well, you want to scale.

00:03:23.910 --> 00:03:28.670
But to put a finer point on
it, there's really two reasons.

00:03:28.670 --> 00:03:32.990
One is that your parameters
or activations or gradients

00:03:32.990 --> 00:03:37.890
and optimizer state don't fit on
the HBM memory of a single GPU.

00:03:37.890 --> 00:03:40.670
So B21 has 192 gigabytes.

00:03:40.670 --> 00:03:42.930
If you're training a 1
trillion parameter model,

00:03:42.930 --> 00:03:46.070
that's not going to
fit on a single GPU.

00:03:46.070 --> 00:03:49.070
And the other reason is that
even if your model could

00:03:49.070 --> 00:03:51.150
fit on a single
GPU, you might want

00:03:51.150 --> 00:03:54.630
to leverage more GPUs by
splitting everything up

00:03:54.630 --> 00:03:56.510
to train faster.

00:03:56.510 --> 00:03:58.990
So sometimes there
will be some decisions

00:03:58.990 --> 00:04:02.930
to be made because you could
fit everything on GPUs,

00:04:02.930 --> 00:04:06.190
but you have fewer cores.

00:04:06.190 --> 00:04:08.230
But if you spread
it out, then you're

00:04:08.230 --> 00:04:11.550
going to have to pay the
communication bandwidth.

00:04:11.550 --> 00:04:13.110
So that's some
calculation you're

00:04:13.110 --> 00:04:18.470
going to have to do to figure
out how to parallelize.

00:04:18.470 --> 00:04:24.490
So just one note here is that
so far this lecture is Python.

00:04:24.490 --> 00:04:27.350
You execute it and you
can just show everything.

00:04:27.350 --> 00:04:30.550
Now, in this lecture, if
you run this directly,

00:04:30.550 --> 00:04:33.630
it uses multiprocessing.

00:04:33.630 --> 00:04:36.070
But when I trace through
it, I'm putting in some

00:04:36.070 --> 00:04:38.647
of special single process mode.

00:04:38.647 --> 00:04:40.230
So if you want to
see the standard out

00:04:40.230 --> 00:04:45.070
for this lecture run in
the multiprocessing setup,

00:04:45.070 --> 00:04:46.050
you can click here.

00:04:46.050 --> 00:04:48.750
And I'll show this as we
go through the lecture.

00:04:48.750 --> 00:04:51.090
But just remember as we
step through this lecture,

00:04:51.090 --> 00:04:53.150
we're not actually
doing multiprocessing,

00:04:53.150 --> 00:04:56.990
because we're just stepping
through single lines of code.

00:04:56.990 --> 00:05:01.910
So this lecture is going
to include two parts.

00:05:01.910 --> 00:05:04.430
One is we're going to
learn about the building

00:05:04.430 --> 00:05:08.510
blocks of distributed
communication and computation,

00:05:08.510 --> 00:05:10.930
starting with the
programming model.

00:05:10.930 --> 00:05:13.430
Talk a little bit
about the hardware.

00:05:13.430 --> 00:05:16.070
Start to implement
things in Torch,

00:05:16.070 --> 00:05:20.790
which you're going to do
on your assignment two.

00:05:20.790 --> 00:05:22.750
And then the second
part is we're going

00:05:22.750 --> 00:05:25.470
to look at actual training.

00:05:25.470 --> 00:05:28.430
We're going to look at
three types of parallelism.

00:05:28.430 --> 00:05:30.190
Data parallelism,
tensor parallelism,

00:05:30.190 --> 00:05:31.470
pipeline parallelism.

00:05:31.470 --> 00:05:34.530
Each is going to cut up our
model in different ways.

00:05:34.530 --> 00:05:38.230
We're going to do this
for MLPs rather than

00:05:38.230 --> 00:05:39.490
the full transformer.

00:05:39.490 --> 00:05:42.870
But it's really the
core computation

00:05:42.870 --> 00:05:45.630
is going to be shown here.

00:05:45.630 --> 00:05:50.390
So let's dive in.

00:05:50.390 --> 00:05:53.540
So the first thing to
talk about is these things

00:05:53.540 --> 00:05:56.380
called collective operations.

00:05:56.380 --> 00:05:59.620
So collective operations
are these primitives from

00:05:59.620 --> 00:06:03.200
distributed programming
that go back to the '80s.

00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:07.460
So the idea of parallel
programming is very old.

00:06:07.460 --> 00:06:10.820
It wasn't invented
for LLM training.

00:06:10.820 --> 00:06:14.180
And it's still the case
that these primitives

00:06:14.180 --> 00:06:18.140
are the ones that we use today.

00:06:18.140 --> 00:06:20.140
And here, collective
just means that you're

00:06:20.140 --> 00:06:22.260
specifying a general
communication

00:06:22.260 --> 00:06:25.560
pattern or a template
across multiple devices,

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:28.660
rather than managing
point to point how

00:06:28.660 --> 00:06:32.020
this GPU is going to
communicate with another GPU.

00:06:32.020 --> 00:06:34.360
And this is going
to be much easier,

00:06:34.360 --> 00:06:36.720
and the system can do a
lot more work for you.

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:40.740
So this is a very tried
and trued interface

00:06:40.740 --> 00:06:44.140
for doing parallel programming.

00:06:44.140 --> 00:06:47.580
So the general
setup is as follows.

00:06:47.580 --> 00:06:49.540
The terminology here
is a little bit--

00:06:49.540 --> 00:06:51.860
I find it a little bit
strange, but this is standard

00:06:51.860 --> 00:06:55.100
in parallel programming.

00:06:55.100 --> 00:06:58.740
The idea is that you
have a bunch of ranks

00:06:58.740 --> 00:07:02.020
where rank corresponds to a
particular device, in our case

00:07:02.020 --> 00:07:02.640
a GPU.

00:07:02.640 --> 00:07:03.980
It could be a TPU.

00:07:03.980 --> 00:07:06.780
But the point is that you have,
let's say, four ranks here.

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:09.960
The world size corresponds
to the number of devices,

00:07:09.960 --> 00:07:13.060
so the world size here is four.

00:07:13.060 --> 00:07:17.620
So there is a few operations
we're going to go through.

00:07:17.620 --> 00:07:19.940
Broadcast, scatter,
gather, reduce,

00:07:19.940 --> 00:07:23.940
all gather, reduce scatter,
all reduce, and all to all.

00:07:23.940 --> 00:07:25.820
And each of these
operations is going

00:07:25.820 --> 00:07:29.780
to specify how this
set of ranks or devices

00:07:29.780 --> 00:07:33.020
is going to transfer
some amount of data

00:07:33.020 --> 00:07:37.220
slash compute with it to
some other set of devices.

00:07:37.220 --> 00:07:40.660
So the first three, broadcast,
scatter, gather, reduce,

00:07:40.660 --> 00:07:44.380
are really just warm
ups, I would say.

00:07:44.380 --> 00:07:51.540
They allow you to get a sense of
how these collective operations

00:07:51.540 --> 00:07:53.020
work, but they're
not really going

00:07:53.020 --> 00:07:56.100
to be the ones that are
driving most of training.

00:07:56.100 --> 00:07:57.700
All gather, reduce
scatter, and all

00:07:57.700 --> 00:07:59.620
reduce are the
ones that are going

00:07:59.620 --> 00:08:04.180
to show up again and again for
distributed training of language

00:08:04.180 --> 00:08:05.080
models.

00:08:05.080 --> 00:08:06.460
And finally, all
to all I'll just

00:08:06.460 --> 00:08:11.280
mention here, which
is important for MOEs,

00:08:11.280 --> 00:08:13.700
but we're not going to
actually spend too much time

00:08:13.700 --> 00:08:16.060
on this this lecture.

00:08:16.060 --> 00:08:21.780
OK, so let's dive in with
the simplest operation, which

00:08:21.780 --> 00:08:23.340
is broadcast.

00:08:23.340 --> 00:08:26.920
So in broadcasting,
you have a rank 0.

00:08:26.920 --> 00:08:30.660
It could be any rank, but let's
just say for sake of picking

00:08:30.660 --> 00:08:36.380
one, rank 0, has
some tensor 0123.

00:08:36.380 --> 00:08:39.179
And it broadcasts
it to all the ranks.

00:08:39.179 --> 00:08:44.420
So at the end of this operation,
we have that each of the ranks

00:08:44.420 --> 00:08:48.100
has the same tensor on it.

00:08:48.100 --> 00:08:51.580
That should be pretty
straightforward.

00:08:51.580 --> 00:08:54.300
And this, again,
doesn't really show up

00:08:54.300 --> 00:08:57.100
in the core path of training.

00:08:57.100 --> 00:09:01.200
Generally, broadcasts are
used for initialization,

00:09:01.200 --> 00:09:04.540
where let's say you initialize
a load initial checkpoint

00:09:04.540 --> 00:09:07.140
and then you broadcast
it to all the ranks.

00:09:07.140 --> 00:09:10.260
So something that's
done like once.

00:09:10.260 --> 00:09:15.380
So the second
operation is a scatter.

00:09:15.380 --> 00:09:23.340
And a scatter basically says,
I have a tensor at rank 0

00:09:23.340 --> 00:09:27.260
who is split up
into the world size.

00:09:27.260 --> 00:09:29.900
And I'm going to
basically scatter

00:09:29.900 --> 00:09:32.860
my tensor onto the other ranks.

00:09:32.860 --> 00:09:36.780
So rank 0 gets 0,
the zeroth component.

00:09:36.780 --> 00:09:41.740
Rank 1 gets this, rank 2 gets
this, and rank 3 gets that.

00:09:41.740 --> 00:09:45.740
So again, this is
not directly used,

00:09:45.740 --> 00:09:48.610
but scatter is an
important stepping stone

00:09:48.610 --> 00:09:50.190
to understand reduced scatter.

00:09:50.190 --> 00:09:54.690
So, as the name implies,
scatter just takes a big tensor

00:09:54.690 --> 00:09:59.610
at one place and spreads it
out onto multiple places.

00:09:59.610 --> 00:10:01.810
And you can see how
this might be helpful,

00:10:01.810 --> 00:10:05.930
because you want
all the GPUs you're

00:10:05.930 --> 00:10:09.410
scattering to do some
local computation

00:10:09.410 --> 00:10:10.790
on the different parts.

00:10:13.690 --> 00:10:17.170
So the inverse of
scatter is scatter.

00:10:17.170 --> 00:10:20.010
So this should be
very predictable.

00:10:20.010 --> 00:10:24.170
The input is you have a bunch
of pieces, each of which

00:10:24.170 --> 00:10:26.690
reside on a particular rank.

00:10:26.690 --> 00:10:29.770
And then when you
do a gather, that's

00:10:29.770 --> 00:10:32.810
with respect to a
particular rank, rank 0,

00:10:32.810 --> 00:10:35.430
it's going to just concatenate
all the pieces together.

00:10:38.170 --> 00:10:40.510
Again, gather isn't
directly used,

00:10:40.510 --> 00:10:45.530
but it's going to be a stepping
stone to understand all gather.

00:10:45.530 --> 00:10:47.470
So next is reduce.

00:10:47.470 --> 00:10:50.970
So those of you who do
functional programming are

00:10:50.970 --> 00:10:52.770
probably familiar
with what reduce is.

00:10:52.770 --> 00:10:54.930
It's exactly the same.

00:10:54.930 --> 00:10:58.450
The idea is that you start
in the same starting point

00:10:58.450 --> 00:11:02.330
as a gather.

00:11:02.330 --> 00:11:07.490
And where the tensor is split
up across multiple-- well,

00:11:07.490 --> 00:11:10.930
you have some piece of data on
each of the different ranks.

00:11:10.930 --> 00:11:13.250
And then you're going
to apply your reduction

00:11:13.250 --> 00:11:20.010
operation to all of these and
get that in, put that on rank 0.

00:11:20.010 --> 00:11:23.250
So in this case, if you
do a reduction with sum,

00:11:23.250 --> 00:11:26.810
then you add these
all up, you get six.

00:11:26.810 --> 00:11:29.530
So you can think about
gather as a reduction

00:11:29.530 --> 00:11:34.390
where the operation is
concatenation, if you will.

00:11:37.170 --> 00:11:39.850
And of course, reduce is
important to understanding

00:11:39.850 --> 00:11:41.650
all reduce.

00:11:41.650 --> 00:11:43.810
So let me pause there.

00:11:43.810 --> 00:11:46.290
Those were just the warm
ups. Just in case people

00:11:46.290 --> 00:11:50.850
have any questions about what
a collective operation is,

00:11:50.850 --> 00:11:54.510
broadcast, scatter,
gather, reduce.

00:11:59.850 --> 00:12:01.450
Yeah?

00:12:01.450 --> 00:12:06.250
[INAUDIBLE] NumPy, where is it?

00:12:06.250 --> 00:12:08.290
So the question
is, is this related

00:12:08.290 --> 00:12:12.410
to broadcasting and NumPy?

00:12:12.410 --> 00:12:17.090
I mean, I think it's
conceptually the same idea,

00:12:17.090 --> 00:12:19.650
where you have one thing
that goes to many things.

00:12:19.650 --> 00:12:21.190
Like in NumPy, if
you have a scalar,

00:12:21.190 --> 00:12:22.770
get broadcast to a tensor.

00:12:22.770 --> 00:12:27.670
But the instantiation, this is
for collective communication.

00:12:27.670 --> 00:12:29.030
So it's a bit different.

00:12:32.650 --> 00:12:35.950
So let's move on to
something more interesting.

00:12:35.950 --> 00:12:41.090
So all gather is what
you do to-- basically,

00:12:41.090 --> 00:12:45.160
you perform gather to all
ranks, not just rank 0.

00:12:45.160 --> 00:12:47.040
So remember what gather does.

00:12:47.040 --> 00:12:51.280
It basically takes all
the different pieces

00:12:51.280 --> 00:12:55.480
and it just puts it
on one rank, rank 0.

00:12:55.480 --> 00:13:00.320
And now all gather just does
it for every single rank.

00:13:00.320 --> 00:13:01.900
That's what the all is for.

00:13:01.900 --> 00:13:06.040
All means do it for all the
output to all the ranks,

00:13:06.040 --> 00:13:08.540
and gather is what you're
doing to all the ranks.

00:13:12.560 --> 00:13:16.160
So this is going
to come up a bunch.

00:13:16.160 --> 00:13:18.440
It's not important that you
understand this statement

00:13:18.440 --> 00:13:23.160
precisely, but later we'll
see that each rank holds

00:13:23.160 --> 00:13:25.040
part of the parameters.

00:13:25.040 --> 00:13:27.720
And then what you
need to do is all

00:13:27.720 --> 00:13:30.160
gather the parameters to
get the full parameters

00:13:30.160 --> 00:13:32.160
for the full forward pass.

00:13:32.160 --> 00:13:34.380
So in general, as
we're doing training,

00:13:34.380 --> 00:13:38.160
we're going to see a lot of
this gather to do something

00:13:38.160 --> 00:13:43.040
and then scatter and then
gather and scatter again.

00:13:43.040 --> 00:13:49.720
So reduce scatter is performing
reduce on each dimension

00:13:49.720 --> 00:13:52.800
and then scattering the results.

00:13:52.800 --> 00:14:02.320
So let's say you have four
devices and each of them

00:14:02.320 --> 00:14:05.400
has some vector.

00:14:05.400 --> 00:14:12.260
And so when we did a reduce
before, we just had 0123,

00:14:12.260 --> 00:14:14.520
and that got reduced to 6.

00:14:14.520 --> 00:14:17.720
But now reduce scatter says
that for each component

00:14:17.720 --> 00:14:20.720
of this tensor, I'm
going to do a reduction,

00:14:20.720 --> 00:14:24.620
and then I'm going to put
it on a different rank.

00:14:27.840 --> 00:14:33.625
So the first dimension, I'm
going to add these up, I get 6.

00:14:33.625 --> 00:14:36.000
Now, for the second dimension,
I'm going to add these up.

00:14:36.000 --> 00:14:37.000
I get 10.

00:14:37.000 --> 00:14:39.012
For third dimension, I'm
going to process these.

00:14:39.012 --> 00:14:41.220
For the fourth dimension,
I'm going to process those.

00:14:47.740 --> 00:14:52.440
So where this is going to show
up, just to foreshadow things,

00:14:52.440 --> 00:14:57.440
after the backward pass, when
you sum what you're going to do

00:14:57.440 --> 00:15:02.640
is each GPU will be dealing
with different data.

00:15:02.640 --> 00:15:07.760
And what you're doing is you
need to sum all of the gradients

00:15:07.760 --> 00:15:10.160
from the different shards.

00:15:10.160 --> 00:15:14.820
And then you're going to
redistribute this storage.

00:15:18.360 --> 00:15:21.080
And then finally, all reduce.

00:15:21.080 --> 00:15:25.280
If you understand all reduce
gather and all gather,

00:15:25.280 --> 00:15:28.740
it's basically you do one
and then you do the other.

00:15:28.740 --> 00:15:35.280
So what this does is reduce
scatter, the same input as we

00:15:35.280 --> 00:15:37.640
had before.

00:15:37.640 --> 00:15:41.520
And remember, in
reduce scatter, we

00:15:41.520 --> 00:15:46.120
had 6, 10, 14, and 18
sit on different ranks.

00:15:46.120 --> 00:15:49.280
And the all gather
part of all reduce just

00:15:49.280 --> 00:15:52.040
puts them all on the same--

00:15:52.040 --> 00:15:53.700
everything on the same node.

00:15:56.500 --> 00:15:59.620
So all reduce is in some sense
the easiest to understand.

00:15:59.620 --> 00:16:01.460
You have a bunch of tensors.

00:16:01.460 --> 00:16:03.680
You reduce, in this case, some.

00:16:03.680 --> 00:16:05.940
And then you replicate
them on all the nodes.

00:16:09.400 --> 00:16:12.520
So we're going to see
this one actually first

00:16:12.520 --> 00:16:16.240
when we do a data parallel
where we sum the gradients

00:16:16.240 --> 00:16:20.560
and then we replicate
the full parameters.

00:16:20.560 --> 00:16:22.660
So that's where we're
actually going to start.

00:16:22.660 --> 00:16:25.040
So maybe just focus your
attention on all reduce.

00:16:25.040 --> 00:16:30.800
Later, we're going to see how
to get to fancier things like 0

00:16:30.800 --> 00:16:32.280
or FSDP.

00:16:32.280 --> 00:16:36.440
We need to break the all reduce
into reduce scatter and all

00:16:36.440 --> 00:16:39.190
gather, because then
you can intervene

00:16:39.190 --> 00:16:41.410
and you can manage
things a bit more.

00:16:41.410 --> 00:16:45.530
But for the basic version,
all reduce is fine.

00:16:48.390 --> 00:16:51.710
Finally, all to all.

00:16:51.710 --> 00:16:54.370
This one is, in some
ways, the most general.

00:16:54.370 --> 00:17:00.190
You basically specify how each
rank sends a particular message

00:17:00.190 --> 00:17:03.230
to another rank.

00:17:03.230 --> 00:17:06.710
And so here's a simple
example where you

00:17:06.710 --> 00:17:09.310
have the same input as before.

00:17:09.310 --> 00:17:11.550
And what this is
saying is that I

00:17:11.550 --> 00:17:17.790
want to send 0 to this
element to rank 0,

00:17:17.790 --> 00:17:19.550
meaning keep it myself.

00:17:19.550 --> 00:17:22.829
I'm going to send
one to rank 1, two

00:17:22.829 --> 00:17:25.829
to rank 2, and three to rank 3.

00:17:25.829 --> 00:17:31.150
And then if I'm rank 1, I want
to send four to rank 0, five

00:17:31.150 --> 00:17:36.110
to rank 1, six to rank
2, and seven to rank 3.

00:17:36.110 --> 00:17:39.230
So basically, the
position here is

00:17:39.230 --> 00:17:42.230
going to denote
which rank is going

00:17:42.230 --> 00:17:44.670
to be the ultimate destination.

00:17:44.670 --> 00:17:49.230
And so if you look at
the output, what happens

00:17:49.230 --> 00:17:54.950
is that the first rank is
going to receive everyone

00:17:54.950 --> 00:18:00.390
who sent everything in column
0, because all these ranks are

00:18:00.390 --> 00:18:03.510
sending these things to rank 0.

00:18:03.510 --> 00:18:08.670
Similarly, for rank
1, all of the ranks

00:18:08.670 --> 00:18:12.370
are sending this column to
rank 1, and so on and so forth.

00:18:16.870 --> 00:18:22.710
So this is going to be useful
when for training MOEs.

00:18:22.710 --> 00:18:26.350
And the intuition
here is that each rank

00:18:26.350 --> 00:18:29.990
has both a split of the data
and also a subset of experts.

00:18:29.990 --> 00:18:34.070
And basically, and the
key idea of the MOE

00:18:34.070 --> 00:18:36.170
is that it's dynamic routing.

00:18:36.170 --> 00:18:38.390
You have to look at
your data to figure out

00:18:38.390 --> 00:18:44.630
which experts you need to
route those activations to.

00:18:44.630 --> 00:18:49.950
So it ends up being a
all to all communication.

00:18:49.950 --> 00:18:56.190
So if everything were balanced,
meaning that every rank sent

00:18:56.190 --> 00:19:00.805
the same number of bytes
to every other rank,

00:19:00.805 --> 00:19:02.430
then all to all, you
can think about it

00:19:02.430 --> 00:19:03.942
as essentially a transpose.

00:19:03.942 --> 00:19:06.150
If you think about this as
a matrix, all you're doing

00:19:06.150 --> 00:19:08.950
is transposing that matrix.

00:19:08.950 --> 00:19:13.710
But in general, all to all
also handles unbalanced splits.

00:19:13.710 --> 00:19:16.030
And I'm not showing
this here, but you

00:19:16.030 --> 00:19:19.390
can configure it to
send any number of bytes

00:19:19.390 --> 00:19:23.230
to any other rank.

00:19:23.230 --> 00:19:24.990
But in general,
you want the splits

00:19:24.990 --> 00:19:27.290
to be as balanced as possible.

00:19:27.290 --> 00:19:29.550
So remember
[INAUDIBLE] MOE lecture

00:19:29.550 --> 00:19:32.980
where we had load balancing to
make sure that things were as

00:19:32.980 --> 00:19:34.340
balanced as possible.

00:19:34.340 --> 00:19:38.820
So morally, the ideal
goal is to have all

00:19:38.820 --> 00:19:40.280
to all look kind of like this.

00:19:44.320 --> 00:19:48.700
So just to summarize, maybe a
few helpful tips to remember

00:19:48.700 --> 00:19:52.700
the terminology, because
I just went through quite

00:19:52.700 --> 00:19:56.660
a few different operations.

00:19:56.660 --> 00:20:00.040
So reduce is,
well, it's reduced.

00:20:00.040 --> 00:20:03.660
It performs on some of
associative commutative

00:20:03.660 --> 00:20:04.400
operation.

00:20:04.400 --> 00:20:07.580
Could be some, could
be max, could be min.

00:20:07.580 --> 00:20:09.660
Scatter is the inverse gather.

00:20:09.660 --> 00:20:12.780
Scatter distributes,
gather centralizes.

00:20:12.780 --> 00:20:17.120
And all just means that the
destination is all devices.

00:20:17.120 --> 00:20:23.440
So that explains all
reduce and all gather.

00:20:23.440 --> 00:20:26.300
Let me pause there
to take any questions

00:20:26.300 --> 00:20:28.320
about collective communications.

00:20:31.780 --> 00:20:32.420
Yeah?

00:20:32.420 --> 00:20:37.980
For operations such as gathering
where we basically [INAUDIBLE]

00:20:37.980 --> 00:20:42.300
to rank 0, does that
rank 0 [INAUDIBLE]?

00:20:42.300 --> 00:20:47.680
Is it a particular GPU every
time or can that rank 0 change?

00:20:47.680 --> 00:20:48.180
Yeah.

00:20:48.180 --> 00:20:52.540
So the question is when you
do a gather or a reduce,

00:20:52.540 --> 00:21:00.580
the target where you write the
output, right now I said rank 0.

00:21:00.580 --> 00:21:02.660
You'll see later in
the code, you basically

00:21:02.660 --> 00:21:10.180
specify the GPU ID or the
rank and it goes there.

00:21:10.180 --> 00:21:13.220
So it doesn't have to be
determined way in advance,

00:21:13.220 --> 00:21:15.940
but it has to be
determined basically

00:21:15.940 --> 00:21:18.240
when you execute the call.

00:21:23.340 --> 00:21:24.760
Cool.

00:21:24.760 --> 00:21:27.060
Anything else?

00:21:27.060 --> 00:21:27.980
Yeah.

00:21:27.980 --> 00:21:30.580
These are just conceptual
building blocks, right?

00:21:30.580 --> 00:21:34.940
They're not actually
[INAUDIBLE].

00:21:34.940 --> 00:21:40.060
Are these the
actual [INAUDIBLE]?

00:21:40.060 --> 00:21:42.820
Yeah, so the question is, are
these just conceptual building

00:21:42.820 --> 00:21:44.040
blocks or are they code?

00:21:44.040 --> 00:21:46.740
So I'm showing these right now
as just conceptual building

00:21:46.740 --> 00:21:48.740
blocks, but we'll
very quickly see how

00:21:48.740 --> 00:21:50.320
these are implemented in code.

00:21:53.460 --> 00:21:56.100
So before getting
into the code, I

00:21:56.100 --> 00:21:58.940
want to talk a bit
about the hardware,

00:21:58.940 --> 00:22:01.140
in particular how
GPUs are connected,

00:22:01.140 --> 00:22:04.940
because we already
what's inside a GPU.

00:22:04.940 --> 00:22:10.100
So let's talk about
networking in general.

00:22:10.100 --> 00:22:12.360
So this is a very
classic picture.

00:22:12.360 --> 00:22:17.340
You can tell from this
very old looking image

00:22:17.340 --> 00:22:22.580
that this is how computers,
I mean, generally work.

00:22:22.580 --> 00:22:26.840
So you have this server and
then you have a bunch of CPUs.

00:22:26.840 --> 00:22:31.660
There's a PCIe bus, which you
connect things like your--

00:22:31.660 --> 00:22:35.700
or used to connect things
like your mouse and keyboard.

00:22:35.700 --> 00:22:39.180
And then you have a bunch
of GPUs sitting off of them

00:22:39.180 --> 00:22:40.820
and you have some RAM.

00:22:40.820 --> 00:22:43.060
And then this
computer is connected

00:22:43.060 --> 00:22:47.020
to ethernet to another computer,
and so on and so forth.

00:22:47.020 --> 00:22:50.440
So this is a particular setup.

00:22:50.440 --> 00:22:52.940
It's a particular topology.

00:22:52.940 --> 00:23:00.060
And GPUs on the same node, you
use PCIe to communicate and GPUs

00:23:00.060 --> 00:23:04.340
on different nodes, you have to
go all the way through ethernet.

00:23:04.340 --> 00:23:07.620
So this is like if you
bought your gaming GPU

00:23:07.620 --> 00:23:10.240
and you had hooked it up with
your friend and was like,

00:23:10.240 --> 00:23:12.500
I'm going to train
some big model.

00:23:12.500 --> 00:23:14.160
That's what you
would have to do.

00:23:14.160 --> 00:23:17.540
But if you're really
serious about training,

00:23:17.540 --> 00:23:21.600
then things look more like this.

00:23:21.600 --> 00:23:25.180
This is the picture I
showed in the very beginning

00:23:25.180 --> 00:23:27.210
where you have the
GPU and there's

00:23:27.210 --> 00:23:31.330
something called NVLink and
NVSwitch and InfiniBand.

00:23:31.330 --> 00:23:35.930
So the typical setup is this.

00:23:35.930 --> 00:23:38.030
And these numbers,
eight is typical,

00:23:38.030 --> 00:23:40.930
but this 256 is kind of made up.

00:23:40.930 --> 00:23:44.850
So typically, you have
eight GPUs per node.

00:23:44.850 --> 00:23:52.330
And these are connected via
NVIDIA's NVLink to a switch.

00:23:52.330 --> 00:23:56.850
And just for calibration,
if you use NVLink 5,

00:23:56.850 --> 00:24:00.330
then you're getting
1.8 terabytes

00:24:00.330 --> 00:24:03.250
per second of total bandwidth.

00:24:03.250 --> 00:24:08.790
And remember, HBM for B200
was 8 terabytes per second.

00:24:08.790 --> 00:24:13.970
So it's about 4x slower.

00:24:13.970 --> 00:24:15.970
So I mean, this is
still pretty fast

00:24:15.970 --> 00:24:19.650
if you think about going
between devices, but obviously,

00:24:19.650 --> 00:24:22.590
not as fast as high
bandwidth memory,

00:24:22.590 --> 00:24:28.450
which is much slower than
shared memory or L1 cache.

00:24:28.450 --> 00:24:33.290
So basically, NVLink
connects to the switch,

00:24:33.290 --> 00:24:36.730
which means that from a
programming perspective,

00:24:36.730 --> 00:24:40.830
you can think about GPUs as
connected to any other GPU.

00:24:40.830 --> 00:24:44.250
You go GPU to any other
GPU, and the hardware

00:24:44.250 --> 00:24:47.550
takes care of transmitting
that to the switch,

00:24:47.550 --> 00:24:51.530
and the switch routes it.

00:24:51.530 --> 00:24:56.050
So typically, what you will
also have is that at some point,

00:24:56.050 --> 00:25:02.330
you can't have
NVSwitch and NVLink.

00:25:02.330 --> 00:25:06.010
Because as your
number of GPUs grows,

00:25:06.010 --> 00:25:11.650
then you're going to have to
put these nodes into pods, which

00:25:11.650 --> 00:25:14.490
are connected by InfiniBand.

00:25:14.490 --> 00:25:18.530
And the way InfiniBand
works is that now there's

00:25:18.530 --> 00:25:24.010
a bit more the-- so now the
GPU doesn't connect directly

00:25:24.010 --> 00:25:25.390
to another GPU.

00:25:25.390 --> 00:25:30.890
It has to go through PCIe
and goes through this kind

00:25:30.890 --> 00:25:32.750
of special InfiniBand cable.

00:25:32.750 --> 00:25:39.370
And you see that the speeds
are much, much lower.

00:25:39.370 --> 00:25:42.090
And then finally, if you
run out of InfiniBand

00:25:42.090 --> 00:25:45.770
and you have these
huge pods, then

00:25:45.770 --> 00:25:49.690
you need to connect
them via ethernet.

00:25:49.690 --> 00:25:55.050
And in ethernet, you
have to go through PCIe

00:25:55.050 --> 00:25:58.690
and that actually goes through
the CPU, which as we'll see,

00:25:58.690 --> 00:26:02.810
is even slower.

00:26:02.810 --> 00:26:06.590
So it's kind of analogous to
the kind of memory situation.

00:26:06.590 --> 00:26:11.070
The more nodes you have, then
the slower it's going to be.

00:26:11.070 --> 00:26:18.850
You can't have an NVSwitch
handling 100,000 GPUs.

00:26:18.850 --> 00:26:23.450
So one note is that I
alluded to this bypassing

00:26:23.450 --> 00:26:29.050
the CPU, which is going to be an
important thing from a hardware

00:26:29.050 --> 00:26:31.730
perspective.

00:26:31.730 --> 00:26:35.330
So if you have traditional
ethernet, what happens

00:26:35.330 --> 00:26:40.950
is that the GPU has to talk to
the CPU to get its data copied.

00:26:40.950 --> 00:26:45.370
So it basically has to
copy the data to this.

00:26:45.370 --> 00:26:47.910
The CPU has a kernel
socket buffer.

00:26:47.910 --> 00:26:50.750
Here kernel means
not the GPU kernel,

00:26:50.750 --> 00:26:54.250
but the CPU, traditional
notion of a kernel.

00:26:54.250 --> 00:26:57.430
And then it has to build
some network packets,

00:26:57.430 --> 00:27:00.450
copy to the network interface,
and then ship it over.

00:27:00.450 --> 00:27:05.010
So this generally
introduces a lot of latency.

00:27:05.010 --> 00:27:08.010
And so there's this technology
called Remote Direct Memory

00:27:08.010 --> 00:27:13.570
Access, RDMA, which allows a
GPU to directly write or read

00:27:13.570 --> 00:27:19.450
from another GPU's memory
without using the CPU at all.

00:27:19.450 --> 00:27:23.720
So obviously, if you're in
NVLink land and NVSwitch land,

00:27:23.720 --> 00:27:26.000
then you have RDMA.

00:27:26.000 --> 00:27:28.840
InfiniBand also supports RDMA.

00:27:28.840 --> 00:27:30.760
So if you're connected
via InfiniBand,

00:27:30.760 --> 00:27:34.440
then you can directly have
GPUs connect to each other

00:27:34.440 --> 00:27:37.880
without involving the CPU.

00:27:37.880 --> 00:27:42.360
But standard ethernet does not.

00:27:42.360 --> 00:27:45.960
There's two notable
advancements I will mention.

00:27:48.960 --> 00:27:51.280
So NVIDIA has been
really pushing

00:27:51.280 --> 00:27:54.940
the limits on what you can do
with larger and larger pods.

00:27:54.940 --> 00:27:59.880
So they have four of basically
the B200's and the B300's.

00:27:59.880 --> 00:28:02.360
They have something
called NVL72,

00:28:02.360 --> 00:28:06.960
which means that they have
these trays of eight GPUs

00:28:06.960 --> 00:28:08.340
but have nine of them.

00:28:08.340 --> 00:28:10.020
And so basically, at
the end of the day,

00:28:10.020 --> 00:28:13.720
you have 72 GPUs that are
all kind of NV switched

00:28:13.720 --> 00:28:15.220
into one NVLink domain.

00:28:15.220 --> 00:28:22.080
And if you remember, the NV
link speeds are very fast.

00:28:22.080 --> 00:28:26.320
So normally, if
you're mortal, you

00:28:26.320 --> 00:28:29.320
think, well OK, I
have eight GPUs that

00:28:29.320 --> 00:28:31.060
are interlinked really fast.

00:28:31.060 --> 00:28:35.182
And then outside of that, then
things get slowed down a lot.

00:28:35.182 --> 00:28:36.640
But if you have a
lot of money, you

00:28:36.640 --> 00:28:39.040
can buy this really
fancy hardware

00:28:39.040 --> 00:28:46.182
and you can get really fast
interconnects up to 72 GPUs.

00:28:46.182 --> 00:28:47.640
And the other thing
I'll mention is

00:28:47.640 --> 00:28:51.680
that I said standard ethernet
doesn't support RDMA,

00:28:51.680 --> 00:28:55.100
but there has been progress
on the ethernet front as well.

00:28:55.100 --> 00:28:59.240
So there's something
called RoCE, RDMA

00:28:59.240 --> 00:29:02.840
over converged ethernet,
where the ethernet actually

00:29:02.840 --> 00:29:05.840
bypasses the CPU.

00:29:05.840 --> 00:29:10.660
And this is sort of their
answer to InfiniBand.

00:29:10.660 --> 00:29:13.600
So InfiniBand generally
is very expensive,

00:29:13.600 --> 00:29:17.040
as is a lot of NVIDIA products.

00:29:17.040 --> 00:29:21.880
But you can get pretty
good performance

00:29:21.880 --> 00:29:29.680
by using RDMA over
converged ethernet.

00:29:29.680 --> 00:29:35.020
And Meta had some paper showing
that they were exploring this.

00:29:35.020 --> 00:29:38.480
So [INAUDIBLE] may or
may not have been trained

00:29:38.480 --> 00:29:40.780
over converged ethernet.

00:29:43.380 --> 00:29:47.540
So that's just a brief overview
of what the hardware looks like.

00:29:47.540 --> 00:29:48.620
You have GPUs.

00:29:48.620 --> 00:29:53.800
They're connected via NVLink
to NVSwitch over some domain,

00:29:53.800 --> 00:29:55.440
maybe 8, maybe 72.

00:29:55.440 --> 00:29:58.920
And then it's
InfiniBand from there.

00:29:58.920 --> 00:30:03.120
And now let's talk about
how do you program this.

00:30:03.120 --> 00:30:05.840
So at the very lowest level,
there's something called

00:30:05.840 --> 00:30:09.000
the NVIDIA Collective
Communications Library,

00:30:09.000 --> 00:30:12.560
NCCL or "nickel," which
translates the collective

00:30:12.560 --> 00:30:17.030
operations, the all
reduce, reduce broadcasts,

00:30:17.030 --> 00:30:20.470
into the actual low level
packets that are sent between

00:30:20.470 --> 00:30:22.230
GPUs.

00:30:22.230 --> 00:30:27.650
So what a NCCL does
is when you use NCCL,

00:30:27.650 --> 00:30:31.070
it's basically like saying,
I want to all reduce.

00:30:31.070 --> 00:30:33.910
And then NCCL goes
and figures out what

00:30:33.910 --> 00:30:36.670
is the topology of the
hardware, figures out

00:30:36.670 --> 00:30:39.470
the path between
different GPUs, and then

00:30:39.470 --> 00:30:42.430
it actually launches the GPU
kernels to send and receive

00:30:42.430 --> 00:30:43.483
data.

00:30:43.483 --> 00:30:45.150
Because at the end
of the day, remember,

00:30:45.150 --> 00:30:47.910
everything that runs
on a GPU is a kernel.

00:30:47.910 --> 00:30:50.870
So there are communication
kernels as well

00:30:50.870 --> 00:30:56.070
that actually do
communication with other GPUs.

00:30:56.070 --> 00:30:59.610
So we're not going to look
too much more into NCCL,

00:30:59.610 --> 00:31:01.890
but just know that it exists.

00:31:04.710 --> 00:31:07.330
And then we're going to
actually go to PyTorch.

00:31:07.330 --> 00:31:13.230
So maybe before that, any
questions about hardware?

00:31:13.230 --> 00:31:14.190
Yeah.

00:31:14.190 --> 00:31:18.710
Can you describe--
there's a rack and a tray.

00:31:21.830 --> 00:31:25.230
Can I describe physically a rack
and a tray, like what a rack is

00:31:25.230 --> 00:31:28.110
and what a tray is?

00:31:28.110 --> 00:31:32.910
So for the NV72, so I'm
not a hardware expert,

00:31:32.910 --> 00:31:37.990
but a rack, I mean,
is literally, I mean,

00:31:37.990 --> 00:31:41.450
you've seen data centers,
literally a rack.

00:31:41.450 --> 00:31:45.230
And each tray is
something that has--

00:31:45.230 --> 00:31:48.250
so the G stands
for a [? great. ?]

00:31:48.250 --> 00:31:50.430
So there's two CPUs.

00:31:50.430 --> 00:31:53.090
And each CPU is
connected to four GPUs.

00:31:53.090 --> 00:31:57.490
So each tray has
eight GPUs on it.

00:31:57.490 --> 00:32:00.110
And they're stacked
and everything

00:32:00.110 --> 00:32:03.090
is connected to this NVSwitch.

00:32:07.070 --> 00:32:07.910
Yeah.

00:32:07.910 --> 00:32:10.062
[INAUDIBLE]

00:32:15.337 --> 00:32:17.670
Yeah, so the question is,
what is the difference between

00:32:17.670 --> 00:32:19.000
[INAUDIBLE].

00:32:19.000 --> 00:32:21.910
[INAUDIBLE]

00:32:21.910 --> 00:32:22.410
Yeah.

00:32:22.410 --> 00:32:27.610
So RDMA, you can think about
it as more of a desiderata.

00:32:27.610 --> 00:32:30.630
RDMA means that one
GPU can read and write

00:32:30.630 --> 00:32:32.710
from another GPU's memory.

00:32:32.710 --> 00:32:35.790
And there's multiple
ways to do RDMA.

00:32:35.790 --> 00:32:39.350
One is to use
NVLink and NVSwitch

00:32:39.350 --> 00:32:42.050
and another way is
to use InfiniBand.

00:32:44.910 --> 00:32:48.710
So InfiniBand and
NVSwitch and NVLink

00:32:48.710 --> 00:32:55.550
are more the hardware, what
pieces, what cables and switches

00:32:55.550 --> 00:32:56.630
are there.

00:32:56.630 --> 00:33:02.590
And RDMA is more
operation, what happens

00:33:02.590 --> 00:33:04.030
when you're communicating.

00:33:04.030 --> 00:33:06.342
[INAUDIBLE]

00:33:15.210 --> 00:33:15.710
Yeah.

00:33:15.710 --> 00:33:18.830
For example, this
[? advancement ?] RDMA

00:33:18.830 --> 00:33:21.850
over converged ethernet
is another way to do RDMA.

00:33:21.850 --> 00:33:23.470
[INAUDIBLE]

00:33:32.610 --> 00:33:33.350
Yeah?

00:33:33.350 --> 00:33:39.470
Is NCCL optimized for
multi-node clusters?

00:33:39.470 --> 00:33:43.350
Because obviously, it seems
intuitive [INAUDIBLE].

00:33:43.350 --> 00:33:50.950
But for RDMA based processes,
would NCCL be optimal for that?

00:33:50.950 --> 00:33:53.510
So the question is,
is NCCL optimized

00:33:53.510 --> 00:33:56.770
for multi-node clusters?

00:33:59.710 --> 00:34:05.750
So I don't know the details
of how they have or have not

00:34:05.750 --> 00:34:06.770
optimized it.

00:34:06.770 --> 00:34:12.380
All I can say is that NVIDIA
has been basically optimizing

00:34:12.380 --> 00:34:16.179
their entire stack for
inference and training

00:34:16.179 --> 00:34:19.500
of these large models, because
their main customers are

00:34:19.500 --> 00:34:23.360
the major providers
of language models.

00:34:23.360 --> 00:34:27.300
So I would be surprised if they
haven't thought of optimizing

00:34:27.300 --> 00:34:29.080
for those type of workloads.

00:34:31.940 --> 00:34:32.820
Yeah?

00:34:32.820 --> 00:34:35.060
[INAUDIBLE]

00:34:44.260 --> 00:34:47.760
So the question is, what
happens if you have nine GPUs?

00:34:47.760 --> 00:34:51.760
How do you distribute the
workload across those?

00:34:55.980 --> 00:34:57.740
I guess it kind
of depends on how

00:34:57.740 --> 00:35:00.740
the nine lands in your setup.

00:35:00.740 --> 00:35:03.180
For example, a lot of
times you'll have--

00:35:03.180 --> 00:35:06.980
let's say you have
eight GPUs per node.

00:35:06.980 --> 00:35:09.340
So then the ninth one would
be on a different node.

00:35:09.340 --> 00:35:14.480
And if you don't have
NVLink connecting them,

00:35:14.480 --> 00:35:16.980
then that's going to be
really bad, because that's

00:35:16.980 --> 00:35:18.900
going to be one node
which is not providing

00:35:18.900 --> 00:35:23.860
that much compute and also very
expensive to communicate with.

00:35:23.860 --> 00:35:27.300
But if you, let's
say, had everything

00:35:27.300 --> 00:35:29.860
were connected by
NVSwitch, then it

00:35:29.860 --> 00:35:33.980
would be much more reasonable.

00:35:33.980 --> 00:35:36.340
One more question and
I'm going to move on.

00:35:39.060 --> 00:35:43.020
So how is this
different from TPUs?

00:35:43.020 --> 00:35:47.420
So [INAUDIBLE] described
TPUs a bit more.

00:35:47.420 --> 00:35:53.220
So I mean, let's see.

00:35:53.220 --> 00:35:56.940
What can I say
quickly about this?

00:35:56.940 --> 00:36:03.180
So TPUs are generally
much simpler objects.

00:36:03.180 --> 00:36:07.460
I'm not too familiar
with the details of what

00:36:07.460 --> 00:36:09.440
each of these components
corresponds to,

00:36:09.440 --> 00:36:11.320
but maybe we can talk
about it offline.

00:36:13.900 --> 00:36:16.460
So let's actually
get down to some code

00:36:16.460 --> 00:36:20.320
to take advantage
of this hardware.

00:36:20.320 --> 00:36:23.500
So PyTorch conveniently
has a torch

00:36:23.500 --> 00:36:25.740
distributed library
that provides

00:36:25.740 --> 00:36:29.540
a clean interface into
these collective operations.

00:36:29.540 --> 00:36:34.140
So you don't have to
explicitly think about NCCL.

00:36:34.140 --> 00:36:38.100
And in fact, this library also
supports different backends

00:36:38.100 --> 00:36:39.660
for different hardware.

00:36:39.660 --> 00:36:43.180
So if you are on GPUs, then
you would use the NCCL backend.

00:36:43.180 --> 00:36:45.260
And if you were on
CPUs, then there's

00:36:45.260 --> 00:36:48.720
something called gloo
which still allows you to--

00:36:48.720 --> 00:36:51.300
like I said, parallel
processing has been around

00:36:51.300 --> 00:36:54.900
for a long time before GPUs.

00:36:54.900 --> 00:37:01.380
And you can do these collect
operations on CPUs as well.

00:37:01.380 --> 00:37:04.140
This library also supports
higher level models

00:37:04.140 --> 00:37:08.050
and algorithms such
as SFTP, but we're not

00:37:08.050 --> 00:37:10.770
going to use those in the course
because we're building things

00:37:10.770 --> 00:37:12.770
from scratch.

00:37:12.770 --> 00:37:13.310
All right.

00:37:13.310 --> 00:37:16.770
So let's walk through
some basic examples

00:37:16.770 --> 00:37:19.570
of collective operations.

00:37:19.570 --> 00:37:22.210
So there's this
function called spawn,

00:37:22.210 --> 00:37:25.130
which takes another
function I'm going to call,

00:37:25.130 --> 00:37:30.210
and it says I'm going to run
this replicated four times where

00:37:30.210 --> 00:37:32.090
four is the world size.

00:37:32.090 --> 00:37:34.770
So let's see what this does.

00:37:34.770 --> 00:37:38.970
This is actually a
wrapper I wrote just

00:37:38.970 --> 00:37:43.130
to hack around the fact that
I can't do multiprocessing

00:37:43.130 --> 00:37:44.770
in this lecture.

00:37:44.770 --> 00:37:47.370
So normally what
you would do is you

00:37:47.370 --> 00:37:53.370
call Torch's
multiprocessing.spawn

00:37:53.370 --> 00:37:56.010
and you call the function.

00:37:56.010 --> 00:38:00.710
But I'm going to do this branch
which disables distributed.

00:38:00.710 --> 00:38:02.650
So let's just go through that.

00:38:02.650 --> 00:38:06.930
So now I'm in this
function that it's

00:38:06.930 --> 00:38:11.850
supposed to be running
asynchronously for each process.

00:38:11.850 --> 00:38:15.010
So remember, world size
is the number of processes

00:38:15.010 --> 00:38:20.210
and the rank is either 0 or 1
or 2 all the way up to world

00:38:20.210 --> 00:38:21.690
size minus 1.

00:38:21.690 --> 00:38:23.450
And so there are
world size number

00:38:23.450 --> 00:38:25.410
of these functions
that are each running

00:38:25.410 --> 00:38:28.170
on a process at the same time.

00:38:28.170 --> 00:38:31.110
So I'm on rank 0 right now.

00:38:31.110 --> 00:38:33.090
So what do I do?

00:38:33.090 --> 00:38:35.010
There's a setup.

00:38:35.010 --> 00:38:41.450
You basically configure the
master address and port.

00:38:41.450 --> 00:38:45.115
Notice that this is not
actually how the GPUs are

00:38:45.115 --> 00:38:45.990
going to communicate.

00:38:45.990 --> 00:38:49.750
This is more for general
metadata and coordination.

00:38:49.750 --> 00:38:51.630
The actual data
goes through NCCL.

00:38:51.630 --> 00:38:54.970
Otherwise, it would
be very, very slow.

00:38:54.970 --> 00:38:57.530
And if you have Cuda
available, then you

00:38:57.530 --> 00:38:59.610
can use the NCCL backend.

00:38:59.610 --> 00:39:04.250
I'm on my laptop, so I'm
going to use the gloo backend.

00:39:04.250 --> 00:39:08.690
OK, so now I'm here.

00:39:08.690 --> 00:39:13.050
Pretend I have four of
these processes running.

00:39:13.050 --> 00:39:19.050
So there's this barrier
function, which is useful as--

00:39:19.050 --> 00:39:20.510
it's a synchronization barrier.

00:39:20.510 --> 00:39:24.410
So basically, if I
see this, then it

00:39:24.410 --> 00:39:27.450
waits for all the processes
to get to this point.

00:39:30.410 --> 00:39:33.570
So basically, you can think
about all the processes

00:39:33.570 --> 00:39:35.687
are running asynchronously.

00:39:35.687 --> 00:39:36.770
So I don't really control.

00:39:36.770 --> 00:39:39.070
One could completely
finish before the other.

00:39:39.070 --> 00:39:42.330
They might finish
interleaved in any way.

00:39:42.330 --> 00:39:45.130
So if I want to make
sure that there's

00:39:45.130 --> 00:39:48.890
some code that is executed
before other code,

00:39:48.890 --> 00:39:52.490
I put these synchronization
barriers in.

00:39:52.490 --> 00:39:54.850
So now the downside of
putting more barriers in

00:39:54.850 --> 00:39:58.490
is that, well, you end up
kind of waiting potentially

00:39:58.490 --> 00:40:01.790
unnecessarily.

00:40:01.790 --> 00:40:04.610
So let's try an all reduce.

00:40:04.610 --> 00:40:11.090
So I'm going to create
this tensor, 0, 1, 2, 3.

00:40:11.090 --> 00:40:13.795
And I have my rank.

00:40:13.795 --> 00:40:15.170
Just to make it
more interesting,

00:40:15.170 --> 00:40:20.530
each rank is going to
have a different tensor.

00:40:20.530 --> 00:40:23.710
I'm going to print out what
I have before the all reduce.

00:40:23.710 --> 00:40:27.410
So now I'm going to
skip over here and see

00:40:27.410 --> 00:40:30.490
what gets printed out.

00:40:30.490 --> 00:40:36.050
So rank 0 before all
reduce has 0, 1, 2, 3.

00:40:36.050 --> 00:40:38.310
Rank 1 has 1, 2,
3, 4, and so on.

00:40:38.310 --> 00:40:40.770
It's the same example
as I showed before.

00:40:40.770 --> 00:40:44.130
Notice that the print statements
are coming in whatever order

00:40:44.130 --> 00:40:47.250
the hardware feels like it,
because it's running async.

00:40:47.250 --> 00:40:48.630
But all the data is there.

00:40:51.170 --> 00:40:57.410
So now if I do all reduce,
and this you pass in--

00:40:57.410 --> 00:40:58.630
this is a PyTorch function.

00:40:58.630 --> 00:41:01.600
It passes in this tensor.

00:41:01.600 --> 00:41:04.920
You pass in the reduction
operation, which is a sum.

00:41:04.920 --> 00:41:08.480
And I'm going to
say don't do async.

00:41:08.480 --> 00:41:12.520
And what this does is it calls,
in this case, it would be gloo,

00:41:12.520 --> 00:41:15.560
but it could be NCCL,
in which case, it

00:41:15.560 --> 00:41:18.020
would spin up the Cuda kernels.

00:41:18.020 --> 00:41:19.380
It would do the communication.

00:41:19.380 --> 00:41:21.880
It takes care of
everything for you.

00:41:21.880 --> 00:41:26.000
And then it basically
writes in place to the data.

00:41:26.000 --> 00:41:31.380
So after the all reduce, I
have the all reduce operation,

00:41:31.380 --> 00:41:34.360
remember, which is the sum
of each of these columns

00:41:34.360 --> 00:41:36.500
but replicated
across all the ranks.

00:41:40.920 --> 00:41:44.800
So that's all reduce.

00:41:44.800 --> 00:41:48.920
And if you wanted to be
fancier and do async,

00:41:48.920 --> 00:41:51.160
then you could say
async equals true.

00:41:51.160 --> 00:41:53.420
But then it would screw up
all these print statements.

00:41:53.420 --> 00:41:56.560
So I'm trying to put more
barriers than I would normally

00:41:56.560 --> 00:41:59.000
would.

00:41:59.000 --> 00:42:01.040
Yeah, question?

00:42:01.040 --> 00:42:03.952
[INAUDIBLE]

00:42:08.560 --> 00:42:11.600
So the question is
the rank the GPU.

00:42:11.600 --> 00:42:15.180
For this class, the
rank is the GPU.

00:42:19.340 --> 00:42:22.840
So let's try another
example here.

00:42:22.840 --> 00:42:26.160
So I'm going to do
a reduce scatter.

00:42:26.160 --> 00:42:30.920
So here, I'm going to
create an input, which

00:42:30.920 --> 00:42:36.080
is 0 through the world size.

00:42:36.080 --> 00:42:44.400
And I'm going to
have the output.

00:42:44.400 --> 00:42:46.440
I'm going to
allocate the output.

00:42:46.440 --> 00:42:49.880
And so what does this look like
before I do the reduce scatter?

00:42:49.880 --> 00:42:59.400
It looks like, let's see,
this, where it's the same input

00:42:59.400 --> 00:43:01.380
and the output
happens to be zeros,

00:43:01.380 --> 00:43:04.640
but it could be just anything.

00:43:04.640 --> 00:43:08.680
And then I do the
reduce scatter tensor.

00:43:08.680 --> 00:43:10.080
Here instead of
writing in place,

00:43:10.080 --> 00:43:13.200
I have an output tensor
and an input tensor.

00:43:13.200 --> 00:43:16.520
I say I want to do
the sum and then

00:43:16.520 --> 00:43:21.700
afterwards, I get basically
the input is not touched,

00:43:21.700 --> 00:43:25.640
but the output I
get the reduction

00:43:25.640 --> 00:43:28.900
of each component written
into the respective ranks.

00:43:36.880 --> 00:43:41.040
So yeah, question?

00:43:41.040 --> 00:43:43.830
[INAUDIBLE]

00:43:51.040 --> 00:43:52.800
So the question is,
how does it work

00:43:52.800 --> 00:43:55.700
to do the all
reduces asynchronous?

00:43:55.700 --> 00:44:01.340
So what this means is that this
is like a monolithic operation.

00:44:01.340 --> 00:44:03.320
You say go do the all reduce.

00:44:03.320 --> 00:44:06.020
And it's spinning
up Cuda kernels.

00:44:06.020 --> 00:44:09.400
It's going to do
the communication.

00:44:09.400 --> 00:44:13.840
And remember, Cuda is already
kind of async with respect

00:44:13.840 --> 00:44:16.400
to the processes,
and now we have

00:44:16.400 --> 00:44:18.360
all the processes being async.

00:44:18.360 --> 00:44:24.800
And the point is that this
code just would return.

00:44:24.800 --> 00:44:29.440
And then you could
do other things.

00:44:29.440 --> 00:44:32.440
So a typical thing,
which I'm not

00:44:32.440 --> 00:44:36.440
going to talk about this class,
is overlapping computation

00:44:36.440 --> 00:44:38.360
and communication.

00:44:38.360 --> 00:44:43.060
So for example, you
can do this operation,

00:44:43.060 --> 00:44:45.560
and then you can go ahead
and load some other data

00:44:45.560 --> 00:44:50.280
for the next step, which is
independent of this operation.

00:44:50.280 --> 00:44:56.130
And then when you want to make
sure that you actually are done,

00:44:56.130 --> 00:44:58.610
then you can call a
weight or a barrier.

00:45:03.990 --> 00:45:08.250
So let's do the final
one, which is all gather.

00:45:08.250 --> 00:45:10.870
So by now, I think
you get the idea.

00:45:10.870 --> 00:45:13.430
Here I'm going to
set as the input

00:45:13.430 --> 00:45:17.230
the output of the
reduce scatter.

00:45:17.230 --> 00:45:20.110
I'm going to allocate an output.

00:45:20.110 --> 00:45:24.050
So before the all gather,
it looks like this.

00:45:24.050 --> 00:45:28.630
I have my results from
the reduce scatter here.

00:45:28.630 --> 00:45:32.250
The output is just allocated.

00:45:32.250 --> 00:45:37.790
It happens to have some values
in it, but don't worry about it.

00:45:37.790 --> 00:45:44.630
And then after I do the
all gather into tensor,

00:45:44.630 --> 00:45:55.190
and then I will have all the
different inputs gathered

00:45:55.190 --> 00:45:59.110
onto all the different ranks.

00:45:59.110 --> 00:46:02.910
And you can see
here that, indeed,

00:46:02.910 --> 00:46:05.190
proof via example
that all reduce

00:46:05.190 --> 00:46:07.970
is equal to reduce
scatter plus all gather.

00:46:13.270 --> 00:46:20.550
And then just to wrap things up,
just as I started by setup, then

00:46:20.550 --> 00:46:27.210
I clean up, which it's
good practice to clean up.

00:46:29.990 --> 00:46:38.070
So that was your first example
of a Torch distributed program.

00:46:38.070 --> 00:46:44.190
So let's do some benchmarking.

00:46:44.190 --> 00:46:49.830
This will be quick, since I want
to actually move on to part two.

00:46:49.830 --> 00:46:52.910
So how fast does
communication happen?

00:46:52.910 --> 00:46:55.510
So let's do an all reduce.

00:46:55.510 --> 00:47:04.550
So here, I'm going to all reduce
with 100 million elements.

00:47:04.550 --> 00:47:05.090
Oops.

00:47:09.910 --> 00:47:11.130
Sorry, I messed this up.

00:47:14.750 --> 00:47:17.110
So all reduce.

00:47:17.110 --> 00:47:21.150
So I'm going to
create this tensor

00:47:21.150 --> 00:47:23.790
with this number of elements.

00:47:23.790 --> 00:47:26.350
And remember, just like before
when we do benchmarking,

00:47:26.350 --> 00:47:28.830
we warm up first.

00:47:28.830 --> 00:47:32.830
And here, I'm going to call
the Cuda synchronize and also

00:47:32.830 --> 00:47:36.750
the barrier, just to make
sure that because there's

00:47:36.750 --> 00:47:39.190
two forms of asynchrony
here, the Cuda kernels

00:47:39.190 --> 00:47:40.408
and the different processes.

00:47:40.408 --> 00:47:41.950
And just want to
make sure everything

00:47:41.950 --> 00:47:49.430
is kind of not running and is
done before I start the time.

00:47:49.430 --> 00:47:53.140
And I'm going to
do the all reduce.

00:47:53.140 --> 00:47:59.620
And then wait again with the
synchronize in the barrier

00:47:59.620 --> 00:48:01.540
and stop the time.

00:48:01.540 --> 00:48:08.940
So remember, this is running
for every single rank.

00:48:08.940 --> 00:48:14.200
So if I look at the output,
here for rank 0, 2, 1, 3,

00:48:14.200 --> 00:48:16.460
I have a different
time potentially,

00:48:16.460 --> 00:48:19.100
because they're all
different processes.

00:48:19.100 --> 00:48:23.398
Each of them is going to
report a certain measurement.

00:48:23.398 --> 00:48:24.940
And if you want to
report one number,

00:48:24.940 --> 00:48:27.240
you can take the
average, for example.

00:48:30.300 --> 00:48:35.240
So now one thing
that is useful to do,

00:48:35.240 --> 00:48:38.060
which is analogous to when
we were computing MFU,

00:48:38.060 --> 00:48:41.900
is to measure the
effective bandwidth.

00:48:41.900 --> 00:48:47.820
And the idea here is that, well,
this took 1.6 milliseconds.

00:48:47.820 --> 00:48:52.700
How much-- is that good or bad?

00:48:52.700 --> 00:48:55.580
So to compute the
effective bandwidth, what

00:48:55.580 --> 00:48:58.220
we're going to do is
to compute essentially

00:48:58.220 --> 00:49:02.340
how many bytes were
sent and should be

00:49:02.340 --> 00:49:05.900
sent during this computation.

00:49:05.900 --> 00:49:08.200
And then if you divide
by the total time,

00:49:08.200 --> 00:49:10.460
then you get the
effective bandwidth.

00:49:10.460 --> 00:49:14.580
So the size of what
I'm sending around

00:49:14.580 --> 00:49:18.720
is the size of each element
times the number of elements.

00:49:18.720 --> 00:49:24.580
So that's basically the number
of bytes of this data tensor.

00:49:24.580 --> 00:49:26.520
How many bytes
get actually sent?

00:49:26.520 --> 00:49:29.620
So this needs some unpacking.

00:49:29.620 --> 00:49:34.900
So for all reduce,
if you think about,

00:49:34.900 --> 00:49:39.460
let's just say for simplicity,
you do a rank 0 plus rank 1

00:49:39.460 --> 00:49:41.820
plus rank 2 plus rank 3.

00:49:41.820 --> 00:49:45.420
You need to iterate this
world size minus 1 steps,

00:49:45.420 --> 00:49:49.820
because there's a world size
minus 1 addition operations.

00:49:49.820 --> 00:49:51.660
So that's this factor.

00:49:51.660 --> 00:49:55.780
There's a 2, because you
need to both send and reduce.

00:49:55.780 --> 00:49:59.780
And then you multiply by
the size of the payload.

00:49:59.780 --> 00:50:03.020
So that's the total
number of sent bytes.

00:50:03.020 --> 00:50:11.780
And the total duration is the
wall clock time that it took.

00:50:11.780 --> 00:50:15.940
And then you multiply
by the world size,

00:50:15.940 --> 00:50:21.340
because it's the total amount
that all the ranks have waited.

00:50:21.340 --> 00:50:26.620
And the bandwidth
is the bytes sent

00:50:26.620 --> 00:50:30.340
divided by the total duration.

00:50:30.340 --> 00:50:33.460
So in this case,
you get something

00:50:33.460 --> 00:50:37.520
like about 400
gigabytes per second.

00:50:40.620 --> 00:50:45.020
So a few notes here.

00:50:45.020 --> 00:50:47.720
One is that the
effective bandwidth,

00:50:47.720 --> 00:50:50.700
if you look at this
expression, size times 2 times

00:50:50.700 --> 00:50:53.800
world size minus 1 divided
by world size times duration.

00:50:53.800 --> 00:50:57.100
So as world size
increases, this world size

00:50:57.100 --> 00:51:00.320
minus 1 divided by world size
essentially converges to 1.

00:51:00.320 --> 00:51:03.300
So you're effectively
left with 2 times the size

00:51:03.300 --> 00:51:05.740
bytes over the duration.

00:51:05.740 --> 00:51:08.920
So this is essentially
the bandwidth.

00:51:08.920 --> 00:51:12.457
Notice that this is
independent of the world size,

00:51:12.457 --> 00:51:13.040
which is good.

00:51:13.040 --> 00:51:16.940
So if you grow the
number of GPUs you have,

00:51:16.940 --> 00:51:19.140
the bandwidth doesn't change.

00:51:19.140 --> 00:51:21.940
It is also independent
of the topology, which

00:51:21.940 --> 00:51:26.260
is something that kind of NCCL
figures out whether you're

00:51:26.260 --> 00:51:30.500
going to pass the messages
in a kind of a ring or a tree

00:51:30.500 --> 00:51:31.360
topology.

00:51:34.060 --> 00:51:37.300
So yeah.

00:51:37.300 --> 00:51:40.580
So that is all reduce.

00:51:40.580 --> 00:51:46.130
And for reduce scatter,
this is very similar.

00:51:46.130 --> 00:51:48.930
So I'm going to create the
inputs and the outputs,

00:51:48.930 --> 00:51:54.170
warm up, perform the
operation, and time it.

00:51:54.170 --> 00:52:02.690
So notice that the reduce
scatter has these timings.

00:52:02.690 --> 00:52:08.090
And you can also measure the
effective bandwidth here,

00:52:08.090 --> 00:52:13.330
the number of bytes
that were in the input.

00:52:13.330 --> 00:52:16.490
You have the number of
bytes that were sent.

00:52:16.490 --> 00:52:20.850
Here there is no 2x here.

00:52:20.850 --> 00:52:23.050
And then the total duration,
you divide by that,

00:52:23.050 --> 00:52:24.150
you get the bandwidth.

00:52:24.150 --> 00:52:28.450
So here, the bandwidth is--

00:52:28.450 --> 00:52:29.930
it should be very similar.

00:52:29.930 --> 00:52:32.090
I guess sometimes there's
some stochasticity,

00:52:32.090 --> 00:52:36.930
but it's in the kind of 400s.

00:52:36.930 --> 00:52:40.090
So a few notes here.

00:52:40.090 --> 00:52:44.530
All reduce is, remember,
as we stated, reduce

00:52:44.530 --> 00:52:47.170
scatter plus an all gather.

00:52:47.170 --> 00:52:54.170
And so all reduce, naturally, is
moving twice the amount of data,

00:52:54.170 --> 00:52:58.890
because reduce scatter has some
cost, all gather has some cost,

00:52:58.890 --> 00:53:02.970
and all reduce is doing
twice as much work.

00:53:02.970 --> 00:53:06.810
And it takes twice
the amount of time.

00:53:06.810 --> 00:53:11.790
But the two cancel out, so you
get the same kind of bandwidth.

00:53:20.150 --> 00:53:22.690
So that is the end of part one.

00:53:22.690 --> 00:53:26.550
Maybe any questions
before I move to part two?

00:53:30.310 --> 00:53:30.810
Yeah.

00:53:30.810 --> 00:53:34.821
Why did you do that
synchronized for [INAUDIBLE]?

00:53:40.455 --> 00:53:43.170
So why do we have to do the
synchronized for the Cuda

00:53:43.170 --> 00:53:44.050
kernel?

00:53:44.050 --> 00:53:49.450
So at the end of the day, we
are still doing Cuda operations.

00:53:49.450 --> 00:53:53.970
We have just multiple
processes, each with a GPU,

00:53:53.970 --> 00:53:57.610
doing some Cuda operations.

00:53:57.610 --> 00:54:01.150
So if you're doing a Cuda
operation, remember, by default,

00:54:01.150 --> 00:54:02.730
it's async.

00:54:02.730 --> 00:54:05.410
So when you reach the
next line in Python,

00:54:05.410 --> 00:54:07.690
that Cuda operation
might not be done.

00:54:07.690 --> 00:54:11.250
So we need to wait
always to make sure it's

00:54:11.250 --> 00:54:13.650
done by synchronizing.

00:54:13.650 --> 00:54:14.850
[INAUDIBLE]

00:54:18.210 --> 00:54:25.100
So do you have to do barrier
first and then synchronize?

00:54:25.100 --> 00:54:27.090
I'm not sure.

00:54:27.090 --> 00:54:27.970
Yeah.

00:54:27.970 --> 00:54:30.210
[INAUDIBLE]

00:54:38.570 --> 00:54:39.070
Yeah.

00:54:39.070 --> 00:54:42.200
I think one problem is
if you barrier first

00:54:42.200 --> 00:54:46.880
and then the Cuda might
not be done running,

00:54:46.880 --> 00:54:49.440
and you just immediately
go to the barrier.

00:54:49.440 --> 00:54:54.240
And then you are still each
independently synchronizing

00:54:54.240 --> 00:54:57.560
the different Cuda kernels,
which means that you're not

00:54:57.560 --> 00:55:00.580
really synchronized.

00:55:00.580 --> 00:55:02.820
If all those
operations just return,

00:55:02.820 --> 00:55:07.080
the barrier doesn't
really do anything.

00:55:07.080 --> 00:55:08.560
OK, let me move on.

00:55:08.560 --> 00:55:10.880
So now let's actually
start thinking

00:55:10.880 --> 00:55:12.960
about how you train models.

00:55:12.960 --> 00:55:15.280
So we're just going to walk
through a very bare bones

00:55:15.280 --> 00:55:19.920
implementation of training
MLPs, multilayer MLPs.

00:55:19.920 --> 00:55:21.140
I guess that's redundant.

00:55:21.140 --> 00:55:24.880
It's multilayer
perceptrons already.

00:55:24.880 --> 00:55:27.680
And remember that
MLPs are the ones that

00:55:27.680 --> 00:55:29.900
are the actual compute
bottleneck in the transformer.

00:55:29.900 --> 00:55:32.480
So this is actually pretty
representative of what

00:55:32.480 --> 00:55:33.760
you'll see.

00:55:33.760 --> 00:55:37.920
So data parallelism,
tensor parallelism,

00:55:37.920 --> 00:55:39.640
and pipeline parallelism.

00:55:39.640 --> 00:55:44.360
And the picture I want
you to have in your head

00:55:44.360 --> 00:55:45.780
is this picture.

00:55:45.780 --> 00:55:49.260
So this is a little
bit of a schematic,

00:55:49.260 --> 00:55:51.420
so don't think too
deeply about this,

00:55:51.420 --> 00:55:55.640
but it's more of a way to
conceptualize how you're cutting

00:55:55.640 --> 00:55:58.080
your data and parameters.

00:55:58.080 --> 00:56:01.560
So data parallelism
says, I'm going

00:56:01.560 --> 00:56:04.760
to split the data into pieces.

00:56:04.760 --> 00:56:08.520
And then each of
the GPUs is going

00:56:08.520 --> 00:56:10.700
to be responsible
for part of the data,

00:56:10.700 --> 00:56:14.200
and I'm going to
just do normal--

00:56:14.200 --> 00:56:16.360
I'm going to keep track
of all the parameters

00:56:16.360 --> 00:56:20.640
and do normal model training.

00:56:20.640 --> 00:56:22.280
And then I need to synchronize.

00:56:22.280 --> 00:56:24.900
So let me explain
how this works.

00:56:24.900 --> 00:56:27.300
So I'm going to generate
some sample data.

00:56:27.300 --> 00:56:29.740
So there's a batch size of 128.

00:56:29.740 --> 00:56:32.480
Number of dimensions, 1,024.

00:56:32.480 --> 00:56:38.400
So this is a batch size by
num dim matrix, data matrix.

00:56:38.400 --> 00:56:44.480
And let's jump into
this data parallelism.

00:56:44.480 --> 00:56:48.600
So the way that
it's going to work

00:56:48.600 --> 00:56:51.160
is that you have
this data matrix.

00:56:51.160 --> 00:57:00.680
And I'm going to break up the
rows into world size pieces.

00:57:00.680 --> 00:57:02.240
And in this case, four.

00:57:02.240 --> 00:57:06.760
And each rank is
going to get a piece.

00:57:06.760 --> 00:57:09.380
So the number of
dimensions, the batch size.

00:57:12.120 --> 00:57:18.600
So I'm going to call the local
batch size, basically the batch

00:57:18.600 --> 00:57:20.360
size divided by the
world size, which

00:57:20.360 --> 00:57:29.240
is every row that GPU sees, it's
going to have 32 data points.

00:57:29.240 --> 00:57:30.640
And this is just indexing.

00:57:30.640 --> 00:57:37.840
Start index data start to end
gets you the slice of that data.

00:57:37.840 --> 00:57:42.720
And I'm going to just
put it on that rank.

00:57:42.720 --> 00:57:46.720
So at this point,
each GPU has now

00:57:46.720 --> 00:57:49.840
a distinct data tensor,
which is the part

00:57:49.840 --> 00:57:52.920
that they're responsible for.

00:57:52.920 --> 00:57:55.200
Now, in practice, each
rank should probably

00:57:55.200 --> 00:57:58.700
load its own data rather
than have this bottleneck,

00:57:58.700 --> 00:58:01.420
but this is just for
illustrative purposes.

00:58:04.600 --> 00:58:08.480
So let's instantiate the MLP.

00:58:08.480 --> 00:58:15.960
So here, I assume
we have layers.

00:58:15.960 --> 00:58:19.560
And num layers.

00:58:19.560 --> 00:58:23.200
And for each of the layers,
I'm going to have just a num

00:58:23.200 --> 00:58:27.760
dim by num dim matrix.

00:58:27.760 --> 00:58:32.360
So I'm just going to initialize
a random set of parameters.

00:58:32.360 --> 00:58:36.910
And then I'm going to
feed that into optimizer.

00:58:36.910 --> 00:58:39.590
So here's the training loop.

00:58:39.590 --> 00:58:42.270
So in the forward
pass, I take the data.

00:58:42.270 --> 00:58:45.810
So remember, data
is not all the data.

00:58:45.810 --> 00:58:50.990
It's just if I'm rank 2, then
I only get B2 part of the data.

00:58:50.990 --> 00:58:54.550
I'm going to just go
through the number of layers

00:58:54.550 --> 00:58:57.430
and do a forward pass.

00:58:57.430 --> 00:59:00.470
And then I'm going to
do a backward pass.

00:59:00.470 --> 00:59:03.590
And then normally,
this would be it.

00:59:03.590 --> 00:59:09.390
But now, remember, every
rank has different data.

00:59:09.390 --> 00:59:15.310
Therefore, the gradients are
going to be different as well.

00:59:15.310 --> 00:59:19.443
So this is the key step that
makes data parallelism work.

00:59:19.443 --> 00:59:21.110
We're going to
synchronize the gradients

00:59:21.110 --> 00:59:22.068
across all the workers.

00:59:22.068 --> 00:59:24.277
This is the only difference
between standard training

00:59:24.277 --> 00:59:25.070
and DDP.

00:59:25.070 --> 00:59:27.170
It's actually pretty
nice and elegant.

00:59:27.170 --> 00:59:29.470
So basically, for
all the parameters,

00:59:29.470 --> 00:59:34.630
I'm going to do an all
reduce of parem.grad.

00:59:37.190 --> 00:59:40.110
And I'm going to average.

00:59:40.110 --> 00:59:47.610
And then after this all reduce
is done, then now at this point,

00:59:47.610 --> 00:59:53.030
each of the ranks has
the exact same gradients.

00:59:53.030 --> 00:59:59.350
And then I'm just going
to update the parameters.

00:59:59.350 --> 01:00:03.990
So it's kind of really
elegant, I find,

01:00:03.990 --> 01:00:07.590
because it's basically standard
training where you apply it

01:00:07.590 --> 01:00:11.390
to your local batch,
but you just insert this

01:00:11.390 --> 01:00:12.850
after the backward pass.

01:00:12.850 --> 01:00:16.370
Let's just average all the
gradients via this all reduce.

01:00:16.370 --> 01:00:22.370
It's a one line code change, and
then the parameters get updated.

01:00:22.370 --> 01:00:27.550
So as you're training,
each rank is basically

01:00:27.550 --> 01:00:32.130
performing parameter updates as
if it had all the data on it,

01:00:32.130 --> 01:00:36.110
but it's only actually
processing a part of the data.

01:00:36.110 --> 01:00:43.750
So that's basically DDP or the
first type of data parallel.

01:00:43.750 --> 01:00:47.110
Any questions about this?

01:00:47.110 --> 01:00:48.483
Yeah.

01:00:48.483 --> 01:00:52.003
[INAUDIBLE]

01:00:52.003 --> 01:00:53.670
So the question is,
can you only do this

01:00:53.670 --> 01:00:57.450
with batch size greater than 1?

01:00:57.450 --> 01:00:57.950
Yes.

01:00:57.950 --> 01:01:02.310
So your batch size has to be
at least world size for this

01:01:02.310 --> 01:01:03.890
to really make sense.

01:01:03.890 --> 01:01:08.670
And usually, it should
probably be quite a bit larger.

01:01:08.670 --> 01:01:09.610
Yeah?

01:01:09.610 --> 01:01:12.850
[INAUDIBLE]

01:01:12.850 --> 01:01:13.350
Yeah.

01:01:13.350 --> 01:01:16.430
So the question is,
should the batch size

01:01:16.430 --> 01:01:17.850
be a multiple of world size?

01:01:17.850 --> 01:01:20.510
And that also would be nice.

01:01:20.510 --> 01:01:22.070
Yes.

01:01:22.070 --> 01:01:23.830
I mean, if it's not,
then you can pad it

01:01:23.830 --> 01:01:25.690
with zeros or something.

01:01:25.690 --> 01:01:31.590
So there's ways, but it's just
easier for everyone if it is.

01:01:31.590 --> 01:01:32.710
[INAUDIBLE]?

01:01:39.870 --> 01:01:44.710
What would it look like for a
transform-based [INAUDIBLE]?

01:01:44.710 --> 01:01:46.670
Yeah, so the question
is, what would this look

01:01:46.670 --> 01:01:47.890
like for a transformer?

01:01:47.890 --> 01:01:50.990
It would actually be
basically the same.

01:01:50.990 --> 01:01:53.910
The DDP has the nice thing
that is very modular.

01:01:53.910 --> 01:01:55.910
You do the forward pass.

01:01:55.910 --> 01:01:59.250
I mean, DDP just averages
the parameters here.

01:01:59.250 --> 01:02:01.810
It doesn't care what your
forward pass looks like.

01:02:04.470 --> 01:02:08.130
OK, let me move on.

01:02:08.130 --> 01:02:11.470
So that's DDP.

01:02:11.470 --> 01:02:18.510
So just to summarize, the losses
are different across the ranks.

01:02:18.510 --> 01:02:22.870
The gradients are also
initially different.

01:02:22.870 --> 01:02:26.150
But they are all reduced to
be the same across the ranks.

01:02:26.150 --> 01:02:27.830
And therefore,
the parameters all

01:02:27.830 --> 01:02:32.500
remain the same across ranks.

01:02:32.500 --> 01:02:34.660
So next lecture,
[? Tatsu ?] is going

01:02:34.660 --> 01:02:39.580
to talk about fancier data
parallelism, FSDP and 0.

01:02:39.580 --> 01:02:42.940
And the idea there is,
as I've alluded to,

01:02:42.940 --> 01:02:44.560
here we use all reduce.

01:02:44.560 --> 01:02:47.100
It's a very simple
monolithic operation,

01:02:47.100 --> 01:02:50.140
but it does require holding
all the models, parameters

01:02:50.140 --> 01:02:51.380
in memory.

01:02:51.380 --> 01:02:54.140
But what if the model
parameters don't fit in memory?

01:02:54.140 --> 01:02:56.000
Then you're going to
have to be more clever,

01:02:56.000 --> 01:02:59.140
and that's the topic
for the next class.

01:02:59.140 --> 01:03:03.980
So let me talk about
tensor parallelism.

01:03:03.980 --> 01:03:07.700
So here, the idea is we're
going to cut this way

01:03:07.700 --> 01:03:09.200
and we're not going
to cut the data.

01:03:09.200 --> 01:03:16.140
We're going to cut
essentially each layer.

01:03:16.140 --> 01:03:22.660
And so each rank is going
to get part of each layer.

01:03:22.660 --> 01:03:26.260
And generally, this
means that we're

01:03:26.260 --> 01:03:29.080
going to have to
transfer a lot more data.

01:03:29.080 --> 01:03:30.840
We'll discuss this a bit later.

01:03:33.820 --> 01:03:37.740
So what does tensor
parallel look like?

01:03:37.740 --> 01:03:42.840
So we're just going to
assume we have this data.

01:03:42.840 --> 01:03:47.980
Every rank has all the
data just for simplicity.

01:03:47.980 --> 01:03:54.300
And here, remember, the data
is batch size times num dim.

01:03:54.300 --> 01:03:56.140
And I'm going to
define a local num dim

01:03:56.140 --> 01:04:00.660
dim to be-- for
this rank, I only

01:04:00.660 --> 01:04:07.140
am responsible for a
subset of the dimensions.

01:04:07.140 --> 01:04:15.180
So the picture here is that each
model still has all the layers

01:04:15.180 --> 01:04:18.500
here for all the layers,
but the parameters now

01:04:18.500 --> 01:04:24.200
are num dim times local num dim.

01:04:24.200 --> 01:04:26.460
So if this were one
of the parameter

01:04:26.460 --> 01:04:35.020
matrices for one layer, I would
be splitting down the columns.

01:04:35.020 --> 01:04:38.940
So this is also known as
column tensor parallel.

01:04:38.940 --> 01:04:40.900
You can also do it by
rows, but we're not going

01:04:40.900 --> 01:04:43.860
to talk about that right now.

01:04:43.860 --> 01:04:48.300
So now what does the
forward pass look like?

01:04:48.300 --> 01:04:50.340
So I'll go through
all the layers

01:04:50.340 --> 01:04:53.340
and I'm going to
compute activations.

01:04:53.340 --> 01:05:00.700
So I start with x the data.

01:05:00.700 --> 01:05:07.580
And I'm going to access
the parameters at layer.

01:05:07.580 --> 01:05:11.040
And notice that this is only
a slice of the parameters.

01:05:11.040 --> 01:05:16.580
So if I'm on rank 1, I only
get this part of the matrix.

01:05:16.580 --> 01:05:18.200
But I can still proceed.

01:05:18.200 --> 01:05:20.780
I can apply this
non-linearity, because this

01:05:20.780 --> 01:05:23.700
is element wise anyway.

01:05:23.700 --> 01:05:27.770
But now what I'm going
to do is I'm going

01:05:27.770 --> 01:05:32.290
to communicate activations.

01:05:32.290 --> 01:05:35.250
So if I have a
data matrix, rank 1

01:05:35.250 --> 01:05:38.570
has activations for
part of the activations

01:05:38.570 --> 01:05:41.210
for this part of the matrix.

01:05:41.210 --> 01:05:43.930
Rank 1 has part of the
activations for this matrix

01:05:43.930 --> 01:05:45.290
and so on and so forth.

01:05:45.290 --> 01:05:49.330
And I need to basically
put all the activations

01:05:49.330 --> 01:05:52.650
on all the ranks.

01:05:52.650 --> 01:05:54.470
But we know how to do that.

01:05:54.470 --> 01:05:58.990
We introduced all gather
as a collective primitive.

01:05:58.990 --> 01:06:05.050
So this is all the activations.

01:06:05.050 --> 01:06:10.970
So this is the batch size
times local num dim, which is

01:06:10.970 --> 01:06:15.290
the shape of the activations.

01:06:15.290 --> 01:06:20.890
And then I'm doing
an all gather.

01:06:20.890 --> 01:06:23.930
Sorry, so this is the allocating
memory for the activations.

01:06:23.930 --> 01:06:27.870
So x is the actual
part of the activation.

01:06:27.870 --> 01:06:31.050
So this is batch size
times local num dim.

01:06:31.050 --> 01:06:37.530
And all gather says each
rank has x and each rank

01:06:37.530 --> 01:06:41.810
is going to allocate
activations,

01:06:41.810 --> 01:06:44.410
which is a list, one
for each world size.

01:06:44.410 --> 01:06:46.610
And then after the
all gather, x is

01:06:46.610 --> 01:06:50.930
going to be copied into each
of the respective location

01:06:50.930 --> 01:06:54.150
activations.

01:06:54.150 --> 01:06:57.310
So then once I gather
all the activations,

01:06:57.310 --> 01:07:00.890
I concatenate them to form
the full dimensional x, which

01:07:00.890 --> 01:07:02.590
is batch size times num dim.

01:07:08.530 --> 01:07:14.310
Any questions about
column tensor parallel?

01:07:18.130 --> 01:07:21.530
So this is done for every layer.

01:07:21.530 --> 01:07:26.450
We now notice one difference
between data parallel

01:07:26.450 --> 01:07:29.450
is now we have to muck
around with the model.

01:07:29.450 --> 01:07:32.230
Data parallel is very elegant,
because it's splitting by data.

01:07:32.230 --> 01:07:35.490
The model is
treated as a module.

01:07:35.490 --> 01:07:38.890
But now we have to muck
around with the model.

01:07:38.890 --> 01:07:42.290
And this is strongly
leveraging the fact

01:07:42.290 --> 01:07:45.730
that if you want to do
a matrix multiplication,

01:07:45.730 --> 01:07:50.050
you can split it up into
a set of smaller matrix

01:07:50.050 --> 01:07:51.150
multiplications.

01:07:51.150 --> 01:07:52.770
We can do those on
different ranks,

01:07:52.770 --> 01:07:55.310
and then we can
gather the results.

01:07:58.290 --> 01:08:00.050
When it comes to the
back propagation,

01:08:00.050 --> 01:08:08.010
[INAUDIBLE] gradients
are still [INAUDIBLE].

01:08:08.010 --> 01:08:08.650
Yeah.

01:08:08.650 --> 01:08:12.850
So now the question is,
what happens in backprop?

01:08:12.850 --> 01:08:16.490
Now, in the backprop,
you have your activations

01:08:16.490 --> 01:08:22.870
and you have to reduce scatter
to all the different gradients.

01:08:22.870 --> 01:08:25.670
So in some ways, all
gather and reduce scatter

01:08:25.670 --> 01:08:28.290
have this kind of duality,
where in forward, you're

01:08:28.290 --> 01:08:33.490
all gathering, in the backward,
you're reduce scattering.

01:08:33.490 --> 01:08:34.430
Yeah?

01:08:34.430 --> 01:08:36.850
[INAUDIBLE]

01:08:36.850 --> 01:08:39.950
So the question is, is that
done automatically by autograd?

01:08:39.950 --> 01:08:42.010
So none of this--

01:08:42.010 --> 01:08:43.490
well, OK.

01:08:43.490 --> 01:08:46.689
So if you just call .backward,
it's not going to do it,

01:08:46.689 --> 01:08:49.630
because there's no
parallelism in that.

01:08:49.630 --> 01:08:53.130
But PyTorch has all
these things that

01:08:53.130 --> 01:08:54.990
are done automatically for you.

01:08:58.490 --> 01:09:05.850
[INAUDIBLE] To what
extent do I need to change

01:09:05.850 --> 01:09:09.290
my code and [INAUDIBLE]?

01:09:09.290 --> 01:09:14.729
So what is done for you
and versus automatically?

01:09:14.729 --> 01:09:18.880
Here we're managing
things fairly explicitly,

01:09:18.880 --> 01:09:21.740
which means I'm not
doing the backward pass,

01:09:21.740 --> 01:09:25.200
but you would have to manage
and call the reduce scatter

01:09:25.200 --> 01:09:25.939
yourself.

01:09:30.439 --> 01:09:33.997
And that's by design,
because this is 336,

01:09:33.997 --> 01:09:35.580
building language
models from scratch.

01:09:35.580 --> 01:09:38.840
In practice, you probably
wouldn't have to do that.

01:09:38.840 --> 01:09:42.359
OK, so let's do pipeline
parallelism quickly.

01:09:42.359 --> 01:09:44.920
So the idea behind
pipeline parallelism

01:09:44.920 --> 01:09:47.760
is we're going to split
the network this way.

01:09:47.760 --> 01:09:52.800
So each rank is going to get
a subset of the layers now.

01:09:52.800 --> 01:09:56.360
Within each layer, it's going
to get all the dimensions,

01:09:56.360 --> 01:09:59.200
and it's also going
to get all the--

01:09:59.200 --> 01:10:04.080
well, one of the ranks is
going to get all the data.

01:10:04.080 --> 01:10:08.640
Every rank is going to see
all the data in some form.

01:10:08.640 --> 01:10:14.180
So this is the way
it's going to work.

01:10:14.180 --> 01:10:20.100
So we have all the
data, which is, again,

01:10:20.100 --> 01:10:23.320
the batch size times num dim.

01:10:23.320 --> 01:10:26.360
And I'm going to
split up the layers.

01:10:26.360 --> 01:10:29.040
So local num layers is going
to be the number of layers

01:10:29.040 --> 01:10:32.920
that a particular rank
is going to handle.

01:10:32.920 --> 01:10:37.520
And so now I'm going to
have local params, which

01:10:37.520 --> 01:10:40.920
is basically only-- there's
local number of layers

01:10:40.920 --> 01:10:42.320
number of them.

01:10:42.320 --> 01:10:48.040
But within each layer, I'm going
to do the num dim by num dim.

01:10:48.040 --> 01:10:53.760
So one thing, maybe
[INAUDIBLE] will talk more

01:10:53.760 --> 01:10:59.520
about this on Wednesday, is
this idea of micro-batches.

01:10:59.520 --> 01:11:02.680
So maybe I'll just
present this and explain

01:11:02.680 --> 01:11:04.960
why I'm doing things this way.

01:11:04.960 --> 01:11:07.780
So in addition to
splitting up the layers,

01:11:07.780 --> 01:11:10.280
I'm also going to
split up the batch

01:11:10.280 --> 01:11:14.400
into a bunch of micro-batches.

01:11:14.400 --> 01:11:20.640
So if I'm rank 0,
then I get the data,

01:11:20.640 --> 01:11:24.680
and I'm going to chunk it up
into a number of micro-batches.

01:11:24.680 --> 01:11:32.240
And for each micro-batch,
what I'm going to do

01:11:32.240 --> 01:11:39.440
is receive it from
the previous rank,

01:11:39.440 --> 01:11:43.840
and then do the feed forward
pass only on the layers that

01:11:43.840 --> 01:11:45.480
are assigned to this rank.

01:11:45.480 --> 01:11:51.760
And then I'm going to
send to the next rank.

01:11:51.760 --> 01:11:55.240
So here I'm actually using
these receive and send,

01:11:55.240 --> 01:11:57.240
which are pointwise operations.

01:11:57.240 --> 01:12:02.220
So I didn't cover those before,
but they're fairly explanatory.

01:12:02.220 --> 01:12:06.200
This basically says
I'm rank and I'm going

01:12:06.200 --> 01:12:09.280
to send this tensor over to--

01:12:09.280 --> 01:12:14.230
sorry, I'm going to receive
this tensor from rank minus 1.

01:12:14.230 --> 01:12:17.770
And this says I'm going to
send tensor x to rank plus 1.

01:12:28.750 --> 01:12:35.710
So basically, the reason why I'm
talking about micro-batches is--

01:12:35.710 --> 01:12:38.510
and [? Tatsu ?] will talk
more about this on Wednesday--

01:12:38.510 --> 01:12:41.990
is that in pipeline
parallelism, so you have

01:12:41.990 --> 01:12:46.030
one rank that gets the data.

01:12:46.030 --> 01:12:48.670
It processes some of
the layers, and then

01:12:48.670 --> 01:12:53.930
it sends it to the next GPU, and
it process some of the layers.

01:12:53.930 --> 01:12:56.710
And it then sends
it to the next GPU.

01:12:56.710 --> 01:13:00.770
So this is a very natural way
of dividing a deep network.

01:13:00.770 --> 01:13:03.070
But the problem is
that you get these

01:13:03.070 --> 01:13:09.430
what are called pipeline
bubbles, where while you're not

01:13:09.430 --> 01:13:13.190
processing, you're kind of
waiting around for other tensors

01:13:13.190 --> 01:13:14.630
to process.

01:13:14.630 --> 01:13:18.310
And this ends up being
quite inefficient.

01:13:18.310 --> 01:13:22.710
So the idea behind micro-batches
is that you break it up

01:13:22.710 --> 01:13:25.430
into smaller batches, so
you can process it quickly,

01:13:25.430 --> 01:13:26.850
send it on to the next one.

01:13:26.850 --> 01:13:32.470
So this can reduce the
number of pipeline bubbles.

01:13:32.470 --> 01:13:34.390
So the other thing
I will mention

01:13:34.390 --> 01:13:37.630
that is not handled in this
very kind of naive version

01:13:37.630 --> 01:13:41.410
is the idea of overlapping
communication and computation,

01:13:41.410 --> 01:13:45.210
which is actually very important
to pipeline parallelism.

01:13:45.210 --> 01:13:47.390
This basically gives
you the right structure.

01:13:47.390 --> 01:13:53.550
If you put an I before these,
then it becomes kind of async.

01:13:53.550 --> 01:13:58.510
You have to add more
things to manage the code.

01:13:58.510 --> 01:14:02.750
And the idea here is
that you want to be--

01:14:02.750 --> 01:14:07.510
while you're computing
here, you can be

01:14:07.510 --> 01:14:11.270
receiving data or sending data.

01:14:11.270 --> 01:14:14.490
So computation and
communication should overlap.

01:14:14.490 --> 01:14:19.210
So that reduces the amount of
time you actually spend waiting.

01:14:23.190 --> 01:14:30.110
So a few things that are kind
of missing here, which we'll

01:14:30.110 --> 01:14:32.110
hopefully fill in next time.

01:14:32.110 --> 01:14:36.190
So the communication
versus computation overlap,

01:14:36.190 --> 01:14:41.950
which is especially crucial
in pipeline parallelism.

01:14:41.950 --> 01:14:44.370
I didn't mention in
data parallelism,

01:14:44.370 --> 01:14:48.810
this also happens, because
I just did a forward pass

01:14:48.810 --> 01:14:51.650
and then at the end, I'm just
doing all these all reduces.

01:14:51.650 --> 01:14:55.110
But if you're clever,
then on the backward pass,

01:14:55.110 --> 01:14:57.090
as soon as the
gradients are done,

01:14:57.090 --> 01:14:58.775
you can start sending that.

01:14:58.775 --> 01:15:00.150
And that's something
that will be

01:15:00.150 --> 01:15:02.510
explored in the assignment two.

01:15:02.510 --> 01:15:06.910
And this, again, allows you
to just overlap communication

01:15:06.910 --> 01:15:10.630
and computation more.

01:15:10.630 --> 01:15:14.410
We had some questions about
what about general models.

01:15:14.410 --> 01:15:19.270
Again, I think
this MLP gives you

01:15:19.270 --> 01:15:24.310
essentially most
of what you need

01:15:24.310 --> 01:15:27.750
for understanding the basics.

01:15:27.750 --> 01:15:30.970
Some of the larger models just
require a lot more bookkeeping,

01:15:30.970 --> 01:15:36.750
so it's harder to see
the core algorithms.

01:15:36.750 --> 01:15:38.390
There are other
types of parallelism

01:15:38.390 --> 01:15:39.950
that we haven't covered.

01:15:39.950 --> 01:15:43.230
So sequence parallelism
takes a whole sequence

01:15:43.230 --> 01:15:44.990
and chops it up into pieces.

01:15:44.990 --> 01:15:47.110
And that allows you to
parallelize the attention

01:15:47.110 --> 01:15:52.670
computation, expert parallelism,
which allows you to parallelize

01:15:52.670 --> 01:15:54.250
the experts for MOEs.

01:15:54.250 --> 01:15:57.590
And this is where the all to
all that I mentioned comes in.

01:15:57.590 --> 01:16:02.350
And then also
different combinations

01:16:02.350 --> 01:16:06.070
of different parallelization
techniques, which also

01:16:06.070 --> 01:16:10.140
will show up in the assignment.

01:16:10.140 --> 01:16:14.460
So one thing to
note is that which

01:16:14.460 --> 01:16:17.580
parallelism technique
you choose is

01:16:17.580 --> 01:16:21.500
going to be strongly
dependent on the hardware.

01:16:21.500 --> 01:16:23.660
For example, tensor parallelism.

01:16:23.660 --> 01:16:27.460
There's a lot of communication,
because for every layer,

01:16:27.460 --> 01:16:30.220
you need to send all
these activations, which

01:16:30.220 --> 01:16:31.860
are fairly big.

01:16:31.860 --> 01:16:36.220
So generally, tensor parallelism
happens within a node on NVLink

01:16:36.220 --> 01:16:40.740
or where you have
high bandwidth.

01:16:40.740 --> 01:16:42.740
Whereas you wouldn't
do tensor parallelism

01:16:42.740 --> 01:16:46.820
past an NVLink domain.

01:16:46.820 --> 01:16:50.800
Whereas a pipeline parallelism,
you'll see people using it.

01:16:50.800 --> 01:16:58.300
And generally, this can tolerate
much slower interconnects.

01:16:58.300 --> 01:17:02.020
So some of the
decentralized training work

01:17:02.020 --> 01:17:05.580
uses pipeline parallel,
because your nodes are--

01:17:05.580 --> 01:17:09.340
GPUs are halfway
across the world.

01:17:09.340 --> 01:17:14.220
But you wouldn't want to do
tensor parallel in that setting.

01:17:14.220 --> 01:17:18.220
So sometimes when you look
at these combinations,

01:17:18.220 --> 01:17:21.100
it will be tensor
parallel within a node

01:17:21.100 --> 01:17:28.880
and then pipe data
parallel or FSDP

01:17:28.880 --> 01:17:35.420
and then pipeline
parallelism if you need it.

01:17:35.420 --> 01:17:39.460
There's other effects, such
as if you do data parallel,

01:17:39.460 --> 01:17:42.600
you might be able to do
data parallel quite a bit.

01:17:42.600 --> 01:17:45.060
But then you start
hitting to something

01:17:45.060 --> 01:17:47.340
called the critical
batch size, where

01:17:47.340 --> 01:17:49.960
if you start increasing
the batch size too much,

01:17:49.960 --> 01:17:52.100
it doesn't actually
help you, in which case

01:17:52.100 --> 01:17:53.740
you're kind of
wasting your compute

01:17:53.740 --> 01:17:56.160
and then you're better
off using tensor parallel.

01:17:56.160 --> 01:17:58.020
So there's a bunch of
these considerations,

01:17:58.020 --> 01:18:03.340
which we'll talk more about
as we go through the class.

01:18:03.340 --> 01:18:04.700
So final note.

01:18:04.700 --> 01:18:08.060
So TPUs came up a little bit.

01:18:08.060 --> 01:18:14.700
One thing to note is that on
purpose, we are using PyTorch

01:18:14.700 --> 01:18:17.220
and not just using
PyTorch, but really using

01:18:17.220 --> 01:18:21.780
the collective operations
in a very primitive way,

01:18:21.780 --> 01:18:25.660
so you can see mechanically
what's happening.

01:18:25.660 --> 01:18:29.700
Another approach, especially if
you're in [INAUDIBLE] TPU land,

01:18:29.700 --> 01:18:34.540
is that you can simply define
the model and the starting

01:18:34.540 --> 01:18:35.740
strategy.

01:18:35.740 --> 01:18:38.380
And the compiler
actually handles a lot

01:18:38.380 --> 01:18:44.060
of the decision of how to--

01:18:44.060 --> 01:18:47.420
basically, what kind of
communication operations

01:18:47.420 --> 01:18:48.400
you need.

01:18:48.400 --> 01:18:51.180
You basically say,
well, this piece of data

01:18:51.180 --> 01:18:53.760
needs to be here
and here and here.

01:18:53.760 --> 01:18:58.940
And then the compiler does
some magic to figure out what.

01:18:58.940 --> 01:19:00.120
So that's appealing.

01:19:00.120 --> 01:19:04.460
But obviously, it would
take a lot of the joy

01:19:04.460 --> 01:19:09.020
out of actually building
things from scratch.

01:19:09.020 --> 01:19:14.660
Just to summarize, so there's
many ways to parallelize.

01:19:14.660 --> 01:19:19.860
You can cut by data,
cut by tensor or expert,

01:19:19.860 --> 01:19:23.700
cut by pipeline or sequence.

01:19:23.700 --> 01:19:26.460
We looked at data parallelism.

01:19:26.460 --> 01:19:28.180
We only did DDP.

01:19:28.180 --> 01:19:32.140
Next time we'll do FSDP and 0.

01:19:32.140 --> 01:19:34.460
Tensor parallelism,
as I mentioned,

01:19:34.460 --> 01:19:36.820
requires very fast
interconnects.

01:19:36.820 --> 01:19:38.980
Pipeline less so.

01:19:38.980 --> 01:19:42.140
But you need to really work
hard to reduce these pipeline

01:19:42.140 --> 01:19:44.300
bubbles.

01:19:44.300 --> 01:19:47.060
And then maybe at
a high level, we

01:19:47.060 --> 01:19:50.900
see this pattern come up a lot.

01:19:50.900 --> 01:19:56.485
So you can either recompute
or store in memory

01:19:56.485 --> 01:19:57.860
when we were
talking about things

01:19:57.860 --> 01:20:04.370
like activation checkpointing
or when we're working with GPUs.

01:20:04.370 --> 01:20:07.410
Or in this case, you can think
about an extension of this

01:20:07.410 --> 01:20:12.130
as that you can store
on a different GPU.

01:20:12.130 --> 01:20:15.290
From that perspective, you
look at the data parallel.

01:20:15.290 --> 01:20:17.810
You're doing redundant
work in some sense,

01:20:17.810 --> 01:20:23.010
because every rank is actually
updating its parameters

01:20:23.010 --> 01:20:25.130
and keeping track of
all the parameters.

01:20:25.130 --> 01:20:26.890
But the reason
you're doing that is

01:20:26.890 --> 01:20:33.090
that you don't have to move
the optimizer state across.

01:20:33.090 --> 01:20:37.230
So one thing is that
hardware is getting faster.

01:20:37.230 --> 01:20:40.910
But in some sense, we'll
always want bigger models.

01:20:40.910 --> 01:20:44.250
So this idea of having
a hierarchical structure

01:20:44.250 --> 01:20:47.210
will always be there.

01:20:47.210 --> 01:20:49.910
So that's it for today.

01:20:49.910 --> 01:20:54.370
So next Wednesday, [? Tatsu ?]
will do more of a deep dive

01:20:54.370 --> 01:20:57.680
on more parallelism techniques.

